## 1. INTRODUCTION
During the past two decades, porous crystalline metal− organic frameworks (MOFs) and porous coordination polymers $(\mathrm{PCPs})^{12,13}$ constructed by metal ions and organic linkers have been intensively investigated in the various applications of interest, such as gas storage/separation,14−16 catalysts,17−20 drug delivery,21−23 and ionic conductors24−27 because of their high porosity, large internal surface area, and tunability. In particular, MOFs with a large number of structural candidates $\left(>70000\right)$ can be a good potential material for a better understanding of proton conductivity and conducting pathways. Compared with other conventional solid-state proton-conductive materials, MOFs have high designability and tunability in structure and property with various design strategies. It is advantageous to create a unique solid-state proton conductor. Furthermore, the high crystallinity and void space of MOFs are beneficial for understanding the conducting path and impregnation of protonconducting media such as water,28,29 nonvolatile acid,30,31 and protic organic molecules32,33 exhibiting unique molecular dynamics (MD), respectively. The proton-conductive MOF was first investigated by Kanda in 1979,34 followed by Kitagawa and Nagao.35,36 The latter authors demonstrated proton/ electron conductivity in copper dithiooxamidate $\big(\big(\mathrm{HOC}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\big)_{2}\mathrm{dtoaCu}\big)$ and its derivatives, although it was difficult to identify the conducting pathway in the pore structure due to the low crystallinity. Since then, much progress has been achieved in this field.29,37−44 In most cases of proton-conductive MOFs, the proton diffuses through the H-bonding network between the frameworks and guest molecules. Therefore, typical designs for proton-conductive MOFs are focused on the efficient
H-bonding formation and increase of mobile protons (charge carrier density). In particular, the target operating temperature is an important factor for conducting media impregnation. For example, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ guest, a major conducting medium in SSPCs, works at the lower temperature region $(<100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , whereas nonvolatile acids and protic organic molecules with a high boiling point (BP) are used as high-temperature conducting media.30,45,46 According to the strategies for achieving protonconductive MOFs in previous reports, proton-conductive MOFs can be classified into five types depending on the proton source and hopping site (Figure 1).29,42 Figure 1 summarizes all the types of proton-conductive MOFs. Type 1, counterion inclusion such as hydronium $\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}^{+}\right)$ , ammonium $\mathrm{\left(NH}_{4}^{+}\right.\mathrm{}$ , $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+},$ , or anion $(\dot{\mathrm{SO}_{4}}^{2-})$ , is simultaneously formed during the MOF synthesis, resulting in the charged frameworks. $^{28,47-49}$ The counterions form the H-bonding with the guest water or the framework, representing the continuous H-bond network with efficient proton conduction. Types 2 and 3 consist of functionalized structural components such as noncoordinated functional groups in organic ligands (e.g., $-\mathrm{OH},$ , $\mathrm{-NH}_{2},$ −COOH, $-\mathrm{{SO}}_{3}\mathrm{{H}},$ and $\mathrm{-}\mathrm{\bar{P}O}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{,}$ ) or coordinative insertion of functional molecules in metal centers ( $\mathrm{~\cdot~H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ , EtOH, and imidazole); these are achieved by two kinds of processes, i.e., employing predesigned components50,51 or postsynthetic modification. $^{31,33,52-54}$ Type 4, the inclusion of functional guest molecules such as acid molecules, protic organic molecules, polyoxometalate (POM), or metal−organic polyhedra (MOP) molecules, constitutes the simplest method of using MOF porosity. $^{30,31,\overleftarrow{5}5-57}$ However, for high performance, extraordinary structural stability is required. Type 5 consists of the intrinsic and extrinsic defective MOFs for proton conduction.58−60 The defective sites facilitate proton diffusion by creating an additional conducting pathway. Although we tried to simplify the proton-conductive MOFs into five types, in fact the reported proton-conductive MOFs are more complicated and cannot be classified into only a single type because the combined components act simultaneously for higher proton conductivity with a synergistic effect.
The general proton conduction mechanisms have been extensively studied using $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ as a proton conducting medium. Considering the proton diffusion motion, two main mechanisms of proton conduction have been acknowledged: the “Grutthuss mechanism” that includes jump diffusion of a proton between the conducting media accompanied by rotational reorientation motion, and the “vehicle mechanism”, where the molecular diffusion of protic species $\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}^{+},\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}\right)$ as a proton-attached vehicle is predominant.61,62 Furthermore, the activation energy $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}\right)$ including the H-bonding formation and dissociation energy penalty, gives an intuitive signal for deducing the conduction mechanism because the Grotthuss $(E_{\mathrm{a}}<0.4~\mathrm{eV})$ and vehicle mechanisms $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}>0.4~\mathrm{eV}\right)$ have a distinct $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ range, which is evaluated by fitting the temperature-dependent conductivity to the Arrhenius equation. Finally, the overall conduction mechanism can be determined by a combination of structural analysis and conductivity and spectroscopic measurements such as solid-state nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy (SSNMR) and quasi-elastic neutron spectroscopy (QENS) with molecular dynamic motion.
Experimentally, proton conductivity is evaluated by alternating current (AC) electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurement in a wide frequency range using a single crystal or pelletized powder.63 The ionic conductivity is expressed using eq 1.
$$
\upsigma=n Z e\upeta
$$
where $\sigma,n,\mathrm{Ze}$ , and $\eta$ are conductivity, carrier concentration, charge of the conducting carrier (proton: $Z=1{\dot{}}$ ), and mobility, respectively. According to eq 1, the charge carrier concentration and mobility can tune the conductivity. From the experimental data, the resistance is obtained from the semicircle of the Nyquist plot from the EIS measurement, and the conductivity $(\dot{\sigma})$ is calculated using eq 2.
$$
\upsigma=\frac{L}{R S}
$$
where $L,S,$ , and $R$ are the sample thickness $\mathrm{(cm)}$ , electrode area $\left(\mathrm{cm}^{2}\right)$ , and impedance, respectively. A more detailed context will be discussed in section 6.1. As mentioned above, when the conductivity is evaluated at a variable temperature (VT), $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ can be estimated from the Arrhenius plot providing an insight into the conducting mechanism. According to thermodynamic theory, the mobility and the self-diffusion coefficient are defined as follows:
$$
\displaystyle\mathfrak{n}=\frac{Z_{\mathrm{e}}D}{k T}
$$
where $D,k,$ and $T$ are the self-diffusion coefficient, Boltzmann constant, and temperature, respectively. The Nernst−Einstein law shows correlation between the diffusion coefficient and conductivity. In porous materials, the diffusion of the conducting carrier is frozen, and the diffusion coefficient represents the random-walk diffusion process, with the hopping frequency, $f,$ and one-step distance, $d$ :
$$
D=\frac{f d^{2}}{6}
$$
The factor $^1/_{6}$ arises from a three-dimensional (3D) unity and can be changed by the dimensionality of the conduction mechanism. Finally, the conductivity with a temperature factor is described by eq 5.
$$
\upsigma={\frac{n Z e^{2}D^{2}}{6k T}}f_{0}\exp(-E_{\mathrm{a}}/k T)={\frac{\upsigma_{0}}{T}}\mathrm{exp}(-E_{\mathrm{a}}/k T)
$$
where the activation energy $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}\right)$ involves the dissociation enthalpy $\left(E_{\mathrm{d}}\right)$ , and the $\sigma_{0}$ is proportional to the concentration of the proton carrier.
Herein, we have briefly introduced the basic concepts and terminology for proton-conductive MOFs. Hereafter, we discuss selective examples of proton-conductive MOFs with various conducting media. Specifically, for a comprehensive understanding, design strategies for proton-conductive MOFs, the performance of proton conduction, the proton-conducting mechanism, and experimental tools for characterization are widely discussed.
## 2. STRUCTURAL STABILITY WITH CONDUCTING MEDIA
For practical applications of the materials, structural stability is an important issue. The structural stability of MOFs has been extensively investigated from thermal and chemical to kinetic and mechanical stability.64−69 In particular, for real-world applications, the structure stability against moisture is on the fundamental checklist due to the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ vapor in the industrial process environment.66,70 With respect to proton conduction, $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ is a good conducting medium in SSPCs because its two proton donor and two acceptor sites form an efficient H-bond network with a degenerated conjugated acid− base system42 $\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}^{+}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\Leftrightarrow\mathrm{H}_{2}\bar{\mathrm{O}}+\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}^{+}\right.$ or $\mathrm{OH}^{-}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\Leftrightarrow$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}+\mathrm{OH}^{-})$ . However, the majority of MOFs are not stable under water vapor due to the dissociation of the present coordination bond by the replacement of organic linkers with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . Therefore, structure stability against conducting media such as $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{\ell}}$ , $\mathrm{NH}_{3},$ and nonvolatile acids ( $\mathrm{.H}_{2}S{\mathrm O}_{4}$ and ${\mathrm{\bar{H}}}_{3}{\mathrm{PO}}_{4},$ which are also detrimental to coordination polymers) is a prerequisite for good proton conductivity.71,72
In general, the simplest way to evaluate the structural stability of crystalline materials is to measure the powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) pattern by comparing the structure change before and after exposure to the adsorbate. Although this method gives an intuitive insight into loss of crystallinity, even some samples retaining diffraction patterns are able to indicate a significant reduction in porosity and surface area. It implies that the partial collapse is difficult to detect in this way. Therefore, the additional information on loss of porosity obtained from $\Nu_{2}$ gas sorption can be conclusive criteria to determine whether the MOFs are stable. Some strategies for the preparation of inert MOFs are suggested,66,73 and all components such as metal species, ligands, functional group, and structure can be factors affecting stability. For instance, the thermodynamically stable MOFs obtain inert metal species that induce an irreversible hydrolysis reaction. In 2011, Kang and coworkers reported on the chemical and thermal stability of isostructural MOFs with different metal centers $(\mathrm{ML}.53(\mathrm{\dot{M}})),$ , where M is Cr, Al, or V (MIL $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ Material from Institute Lavoisier).69 In this case, the chemical stability toward acid, base, and water is in the order of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}53\big(\mathrm{Cr}\big)>\mathrm{MIL}{-}53\big(\mathrm{Al}\big)>\mathrm{MIL}{-}53\big(\mathrm{V}\big),$ corresponding to the inertness of metal ions. Because the oxygen and nitrogen in $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ or $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ are nucleophiles, the guest molecules are able to replace the present ligand in the metal center and deconstruct the MOF structure if the metal center is not sufficiently inert. The thermal stability is in the order of $\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}53(\mathrm{Al})>\mathrm{MIL}\mathrm{-}53(\mathrm{Cr})>\mathrm{MIL}\$ 53(V), where M−oxygen bond strength is a major factor in their trend. A similar trend was observed in MOFs against $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ by Kajiwara et al. in 2014.71 The most stable MOFs against $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ were derived from Al(III), $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})$ , Ti(IV), $\mathrm{Zr(IV)}$ , Tb(III), and $\mathrm{Ln}(\mathrm{III})$ metal cations because the inertness, high oxidation state, ionic radius, and oxophilicity of the metal cation are the factors for high-level stability. Moreover, by reducing the void volume, the short organic linker is favored to form a more stable structure than the longer one. In particular, the Lewis acid−base coordination chemistry is a fundamental principle for MOF construction; thus, the $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ of the ligand gives an intuitive approximation of the metal−ligand bond strength. For example, the azolate-type linkers such as pyrazolate, imidazolate, and triazolate indicate more stable structures than the carboxylate linkers due to greater Lewis basicity generating the azolate anion. $^{70,73-75}$ As a result, the MOFs designed and constructed by these species have superior durability against the polar solvent. Another approach is the introduction of steric hindrance around metal clusters to prohibit the access of guest molecules. The bulky ligand functional group and POM centers reduce the accessible metal sites, which lead to interruption of the reaction between the guest and metal center and dissociation of metal− l i g a n d b o n d s . F o r e x a m p l e , a l t h o u g h U i O - 6 6 $\big(\big[\mathrm{\bar{Z}r}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}\big(\mathrm{OH}\big)_{4}\big(\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{-CO}_{2}\big)_{2}\big]_{12}$ has the low $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ of the carboxylate ligand, the high coordination number of the metal center with oxygen provides high stability under $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ implying high tolerance during hydrolysis. Furthermore, the structural interpenetration and flexibility show a similar effect, obtaining a relatively high water stability through locking a labile organic linker or steric protection.76,77 Finally, the control of hydrophilicity and hydrophobicity, which are strongly related to proton conductivity, can affect the structural stability against the moisture. According to previous results, the hydrophobicity of MOFs can be improved by incorporating hydrophobic fluorinated and alkyl functional groups on the ligand with their water stability.78,79 The structural stability derived from hydrophobicity has two mechanisms, where the hydrophobic group excludes water adsorption or prohibits cluster formation around the metal center.80 In summary, the structural stability of MOFs toward conducting media can be attributed to three factors: metal species (inertness, oxophilicity, oxidation state, and ionic radius), organic linker (coordination bonding strength, length of linker, and hydrophobicity), and steric factor (steric hindrance of the accessible metal center, interpenetration, and flexibility). Although there are some approaches for structural durability of MOFs in previous reports, efficient proton diffusion and H-bonding networks should be considered simultaneously for proton-conductive MOFs, which is still challenging in this field.
## 3. LOW-DIMENSIONAL STRUCTURES AND PROTON CONDUCTIVITY
The designability of MOFs provides an opportunity to control their physical properties. More than 70 000 of MOFs are reported in Cambridge Structural Database (CSD) according to the synthetic strategies such as ligand selection, coordination geometry of metal species, reaction temperature, type of solvent, and concentration.81 In particular, MOFs are of various structural dimensionalities such as the discrete zero-dimensional (subgroup of metal−organic materials such as metal−organic polyhedra), unidimensional (lD, metal−ligand chain), twodimensional (2D, layered structure), and 3D frameworks (Figure 2). Although 3D structures with a void space are most promising for diverse applications using spatial advantage, the low-dimensional structures can exhibit an excellent proton conduction properties via the H-bonding chain because a low void space is advantageous for proton hopping. In this section, we focus on the low dimensional structures and their proton conductivity with representative examples.
## 3.1. 1D Structure
In 2009, Yamada and coworkers reported a high proton conductivity in a 1D ferro-oxalate compound $\big(\mathrm{Fe}\big(\mathrm{ox}\big){\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ $\mathrm{Fe}\left(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\right)\left(\mathrm{\bar{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)_{2}\right)$ (Figure 3).82 The $\mathrm{Fe}\left(\mathrm{ox}\right){\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (Fe was prepared by simple mixing of ferrous sulfate and oxalic acid in water. In fact, ferrous oxalate dihydrate is a well-known mineral named Humboldtine and has a 1D chain structure, where ferrous ions are connected by the oxalate ligand and two water molecules are coordinated on the axial site of the ferrous ion (Figure 3). The coordinated water molecules in the metal centers (Lewis acid) are more acidic than free water molecules and donate protons easily. As a result, the mobile proton in the ordered water array induces proton diffusion through the Hbonding network. The proton conductivity of ferrous oxalate dihydrate was $1.3\times10^{-5}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $98\%$ RH, which is an extremely high value at RT without any strong acid species.7,8,83 Regarding the structure components, there is no potential mobile proton in the oxalate ligand due to the complete coordination. This means that overall, the proton conductivity is attributed to the ordered coordinated water molecules with a distance of $2.71\mathring{\mathrm{A}}.$ In particular, the nanoscale conduction pathway improves the conductivity more than the bulk state in the ionic conductor,84 which can be featured in the coordination polymer due to the long-range ordering of the nanoscale interface. Therefore, the strong interaction between the framework and guest molecules is an important factor in the nanoscale. The activation energy $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}\right)$ estimated by the Arrhenius plot was $0.37~\mathrm{eV},$ , which is a superionic conductor defined by Colomban $\left(>1\times10^{-4}\thinspace\mathrm{S}\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\right.$ and $E_{\mathrm{a}}<0.4\ \mathrm{eV})$ .63 In contrast to proton conductivity, the direct current (DC)
electrical conductivity of ferro-oxalate was very low (insulator; $<10^{-10}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}\big>$ , corresponding to the previous report.85
Morikawa and coworkers reported a Mn-based 1D coordination polymer structure $\mathrm{\small{[Mn(dhbq)(H_{2}O)_{2}};}$ $\mathrm{Mn}\big(\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{2}\big)$ - $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\bar{)}_{2},$ dhbq $=2{,}5$ -dihydroxy-1,4-benzoquinone], its water vapor sorption property, and its proton conductivity in 2009 (Figure 4).86 Initially, the coordination polymers of dhbq were studied by Kanda and some other groups.87,88 $\operatorname{Mn}(\hat{\mathrm{dhbq}})$ - $\mathrm{\big(H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\big)_{2}{\cdot}\big(\mathrm{i}.2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\big)$ was prepared from slow diffusion of aqueous solutions containing manganese sulfate and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{dhbq}$ at RT. The reddish plate-like crystals were obtained where dhbq and divalent Mn ions form a 1D-chain with an alternating arrangement of Mn and dhbq (Figure 4). The compound can be totally activated at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum and adsorbs two equivalents of water molecules in humidified conditions. Although $\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{dhbq}){\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}\left(\mathrm{ox}\right){\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ have a similar crystal structure and packing mode with an H-bond network, the conductivity of $\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{dhbq}){\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ is 1 order of magnitude lower $(4\times10^{-5}\dot{\mathrm{~S~cm}}^{-1})$ than that of $\mathrm{Fe}\left(\mathrm{ox}\right){\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ estimated from the conductivity at various temperatures is $0.26\mathrm{eV},$ , which is lower than that of $\mathrm{\tilde{Fe}}(\cos){\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(0.37\mathrm{eV})$ and is comparable to that of Nafion89 $\mathrm{{'0.24~eV,}}$ , contained $14.4\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ mass in sample). These phenomena are attributed to a weak Lewis acidity of Mn and to the distance between the coordinated $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules and the interligand distance, respectively. The dhbq ligand including the benzene ring has a longer distance than the oxalate. Nevertheless, it shows a shorter interligand distance $(\mathrm{O-O}=2.656\mathring\mathrm{A},$ ) than that of $\mathrm{Fe}\left(\mathrm{ox}\right){\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (2.775 Å). In the Grotthuss mechanism, acidic species and the hopping distance from one site to neighboring molecules are thought to be the factors for efficient proton diffusion. The dependence of proton conductivity in isostructural 1D $\mathrm{M}(\mathrm{dhbq}){\cdot}\mathrm{n}\bar{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\left(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g},\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n},\right.$ Co, Ni, and $Z\mathbf{n}$ ) on metal species was investigated by Yamada et al. in 2010.90 All compounds show the same PXRD pattern with relatively different peak broadening due to the difference of crystal size. The $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ contents included in each compound are determined by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) (Figure 5a).
The $Z\mathrm{n}$ and $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ compounds lose two equivalents of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ before $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , whereas Co and Mg lose 2.5 equiv of $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ before $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The Ni compound shows continuous weight loss up to $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The gradual weight loss of the Ni compound originates from weakly bounded $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ molecules on the axial site of the metal center and lattice $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ between 1D chains. The difference in the water release temperature can be attributed to the Hbonding strength and Lewis acidity in 3d metal species. All of the compounds show a multistep $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ vapor sorption isotherm and have a stable state in the dihydrated condition. Although a higher hydration state was observed in all compounds, there is no peak shift in the PXRD pattern under humidification, which means no change in the alignment of the 1D chain. Proton conductivity depends on the metal species and humidity. Except for the Co compound, the others continuously increase conductivity as humidity increases to $100\%$ RH. These results support that additional water molecules absorbed in the void space in the 1D array contribute to the high conductivity. The Ni compound has higher conductivity than the others in the low humidity range $(40-90\%$ RH) because of the inclusion of three water molecules in $\operatorname{Ni}(\mathrm{dhbq})$ at ambient humidity and stacking disorder as including water molecules.
In 2012, the anhydrous proton conductivity of the 1D ionic plastic crystal $\mathrm{\small{[Zn(HPO_{4})(\bar{H}_{2}P O_{4})_{2}](I m H_{2})_{2}(1)}}$ was achieved by Horike and coworkers.91 “Plastic crystals” mean the longrange ordered structure with short-range disorder. Some ionic $(\mathrm{Li}^{\mp},\ \mathrm{H}^{+})$ conductors belong to this class of material.92,93 In $[\mathrm{Zn(HPO_{4})(H_{2}P O_{4})_{2}}](\mathrm{Im}\bar{\mathrm{H_{2}}})_{2},\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}$ has a tetrahedral coordination with two types of orthophosphates $\left(\mathrm{HPO}_{4}^{2-}\right.$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}{}^{-})$ producing an anionic 1D chain with impregnation of a counter imidazole $\left(\mathrm{Im}{\mathrm{H}_{2}}^{+}\right)$ cation (Figure 6). The orthophosphates and the imidazole $\left(\mathrm{Im}{\mathrm{H}_{2}}^{+}\right)$ cation interact through the H-bond. This compound does not show any weight loss below $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and has the same PXRD pattern at 25 and 140 ${}^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ implying no structural change. The conductivity measurement was carried out under temperature sweeping $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\rightarrow$ $140{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\rightarrow40{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ (Figure $6\ensuremath{\mathrm{b}}$ and $\mathrm{~\boldmath~c~}$ ). A nonlinear conductivity increase (jumping) was observed at $55^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ which is considered as a change in mobile ion motion corresponding to the characteristic behavior of plastic crystals. This phenomenon is derived from the increase in mobility in $\mathrm{Im}{\mathrm{H}_{2}}^{+}$ , which can be supported by the thermal factor increase in single-crystal X-ray analysis at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with an identical cell volume and a more isotropic signal evolving in $^2\mathrm{H}$ NMR analysis.
At ${130}^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the compounds present a high conductivity of 2.6 $\times10^{-4}\mathrm{Scm^{-1}}$ . The calculated $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ from the temperature range of $130{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\rightarrow40{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is $0.47\ \mathrm{eV}_{i}$ , indicating a major contribution of the Grotthuss mechanism. To determine the conducting species, they fabricated a membrane-electrode assembly and observed open-circuit voltage (OCV) of $0.75\mathrm{V}$ for $^\mathrm{1h}$ at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ without humidity. The result is indicative of proton conduction rather than other ionic species $(\mathrm{O}^{2-})$ not produced in the framework. The ionic plastic compound indicates an endothermic peak at around $160~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ without weight loss. It is a melting point exhibiting being viscous and transparent. After cooling the melted state $\left(\mathbf{1}^{\prime}\right)$ , there is no diffraction pattern in PXRD, whereas the scratched fine powder $({\bf{1}}^{\prime\prime})$ shows the same structure as the original, which means that the melted state is metastable. The temperature-dependent conductivity of $\mathbf{{1}^{\prime\prime}}$ is clearly linear $(2.0\times\mathrm{\hat{1}0^{-4}~S~c m^{-1}}$ at $130~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $E_{\mathrm{a}}=0.6~\mathrm{eV},$ ), unlike that of 1 (Figure 6c). The result proposed a strategy for the construction of ionic plastic crystals by various coordination networks.
The 1D nanowire has received much attention in lowdimensional physics due to the electronic state control and various phenomena such as the charge density wave (CDW), soliton, polarons, optical nonlinearity, etc.94,95 In particular, the coordination nanowire compounds have recently received attention due to the systematic control by substitution of structural components. $^{96-98}\mathrm{In}2016\mathrm{\Omega}$ , Otake et al. studied proton conductivity of ultrafine metal−organic nanowires (pseudo-1D structure) constructed by a (Pt) homo- and $\mathrm{(Pd/Pt)}$ heterometallic system.99 The metal−organic nanowires were prepared by two tracks. Homometallic $\left(\mathrm{Pt}\right)$ nanowire [Pt$\mathsf{\bar{(}d a c h)(C N)B r]}_{4}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{4}$ (compound 3, dach $\mathit{\Theta}=\mathit{\Theta}\left(1R,2R\right)$ - $(-)$ -1,2-Diaminocyclohexane $\big(\bar{\mathrm{C}_{6}}\mathrm{H}_{10}\big(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big)_{2}\big)^{\cdot}$ ) was synthesized by connecting a $\mathrm{Pt}$ monomer to form a square ring and then the rings were connected by oxidative polymerization with a halogen (Br) atom, resulting in the metal−halogen bridge along the $b$ direction. By contrast, heterometallic $\mathrm{(\bar{P}d/P t)}$ nanowire [Pd$\mathrm{(dach)]_{2}[P t(d a c h)(C N)_{2}X_{2}]_{2}(N O_{3})_{4}}$ $\mathrm{\dot{X}=C l}$ (compound 4), Br (compound 5), I (compound 6)) is prepared through the direct self-assembly of halogen-coordinated $\mathrm{Pt}$ and Pd monomers (Figure 7).99 The oxidation state of metal species was determined through the single-crystal X-ray analysis, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), Raman spectroscopy, and X-ray diffuse scattering. The homometallic Pt nanowire has $\mathrm{Pt}^{\mathrm{II}}$ and $\mathrm{\dot{Pt}^{I V}}$ , whereas the heterometallic nanowire contains $\mathrm{Pd}^{\mathrm{II}}$ and $\mathrm{Pt}^{\mathrm{{IV}}}$ state. The $\mathrm{x}$ -ray diffuse scattering shows that the 2-fold periodicity of $\mathrm{Pd}^{\mathrm{II}}$ and $\mathrm{Pt}^{\mathrm{{IV}}},$ implying ordered alignment in one nanowire. In diffuse reflectance spectra, intervalence chargetransfer (IVCT) transitions was observed at 2.5, 4.0, 3.0, and 2.2 $\mathrm{^\bulletv}$ (for 3, 4, 5, and 6, respectively) indicating a controllable optical band gap of nanowire. The 1D nanowire shows the Hbond network between the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ , ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}^{-},$ , and $\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ causing an expectation for proton conductivity. Therefore, a single-crystal impedance measurement was performed along the wire direction using Br bridged homo- and heterometallic nanowire (compound 3 and 5).
At $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $95\%$ RH, homometallic nanowire indicates a conductivity of $1.29\times10^{-5}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ $(E_{\mathrm{a}}=0.54~\mathrm{eV})$ , and the heterometallic one has $1.54\times10^{-7}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}=0.43\ \mathrm{eV}\right)$ . Regardless of the isostructural property of both compounds, the conductivity shows a difference by 100 times, which might be the result of a lower optical gap (e.g., lower charge modulation). This study clearly showed control of the electronic state and proton conductivity in a coordination-driven pseudo-1D structure.
## 3.2. 2D Structure
The stacking and sliding motion of 2D layered structures, which have more freedom than 3D structures due to the weakly bound layers, provides an opportunity to modulate the physical properties.100,101 In particular, 2D coordination compounds that are predesigned by ligand shape and coordination geometry of metal species can produce porosity through the stacking manner of ligand $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction.102−104 Therefore, the impregnation of conducting media between the layer and pore direction induces proton conductivity in 2D compounds. In 2009, Sadakiyo and coworkers reported a $Z\mathbf{n}$ -based oxalate 2D MOF for proton conductivity, $(\mathrm{\tilde{N}H_{4}})_{2}(\mathrm{adp})[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ( $\mathrm{\adp=}$ adipic acid; $\mathrm{C_{6}H_{10}O_{4}}$ and $\mathbf{ox}=$ oxalate; $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4},$ ) (Figure 8). In this compound, $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]^{2-}$ forms a 2D infinite layer with a honeycomb structure, and adipic acids, $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}.$ , and guest water are located in the interlayer space with H-bonding networks. There are some short H-bonds between the adp/ox ion (2.688 Å), $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{4}^{+}$ $\left(2.789\mathrm{~\AA~}\right)$ , and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(2.{\bar{6}}38{\mathrm{~\AA}})$ , and its extended H-bonding network indicates a potential for being a proton conductor. The proton conductivity of $(\mathrm{NH}_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{ad}\bar{\mathrm{p}})$ - $\mathrm{\hat{[{Zn}_{2}({o x})_{3}]{\cdot3}{H_{2}O}}}$ is $8\times\mathrm{\dot{1}0^{-3}~S~c m^{-1}}$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $98\%$ RH. However, trihydrated state can be transformed to di- or anhydrate form by the humidity level (dihydrate form below $85\%$ RH).
As expected, the dihydrate form at $70\%$ RH shows a low conductivity of $6\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ , where proton conductivity is strongly affected by hydration conditions due to the change in the H-bond networks. The detailed structural transformation and conducting pathway changes by hydration level were reported in 2014.105 From the temperature-dependent conductivity under $98\%$ RH, the $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ of $\mathbf{\hat{\phi}}(\mathbf{NH_{4}})_{2}(\mathbf{ad\hat{p}})[\mathbf{Zn}_{2}(\mathbf{ox})_{3}]$ · $3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ is $0.63\ \mathrm{\eV},$ , which is higher than that of the typical Grotthuss mechanism because of the presence of half-occupied oxygen sites of guest water O(10) supporting partial directmolecular diffusion. Nevertheless, the efficient proton conductivity is attributed to a high carrier density from counterions, adipic acid with water. These results show the control of the crystalline proton pathways and efficient conductivity through the single-crystal structural analysis. In 2009, Okawa et al. studied the magnetic property and proton conductivity of oxalate-bridged bimetallic frameworks $\{\mathrm{NH(prol)}_{3}\}[\dot{\mathrm{MCr}}-$ $\left(\mathbf{ox}\right)_{3}]$ $\bf\tilde{M}^{\mathrm{~}}=\nabla M\ln^{\mathrm{II}}$ , $\mathrm{Fe^{II},~\thinspace C o^{I I}}$ ; $\mathrm{NH}\left(\mathrm{prol}\right)_{3}{}^{+}\ =\ \mathrm{tri}\left(3\cdot$ hydroxypropyl)ammonium).106 In this honeycomb anion framework, the ${{\mathbf{M}}^{\mathrm{II}}}$ species is surrounded by $\mathrm{\bf\dot{M}}^{\mathrm{III}}.$ , and the combination of ${{\bf{M}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ and $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{III}}$ is able to show ferro-, ferri-, or canted antiferromagnets at the critical temperature. In particular, the bulky hydrophilic cation $[\mathrm{NH}(\mathrm{prol})_{3}]^{+}$ is important for stabilizing the structure. Nevertheless, all frameworks are moisture sensitive and decomposed in water. In single-crystal X-ray analysis for $\{\mathrm{NH(prol)_{3}}\}[\mathrm{MnCr(ox)_{3}}]$ · $2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ the metal species are distinguished by the $\mathbf{M}{-}\mathbf{O}$ distance, and the ammonium cation accompanying the H-bond interaction with a framework is positioned above the $\mathrm{Cr}$ ion. Depending on the countercation bulkiness, the interlayer distance of $\left[{\bf{M}}^{\mathrm{{II}}}{\bf{M}}^{\mathrm{{III}}}{\left({\bf{o x}}\right)_{3}}\right]$ can be tuned. The present NH$\left(\mathrm{prol}\right)_{3}{}^{+}$ shows the shortest interlayer distance of $\bar{7}.90\mathrm{\AA}$ among the complexes. Regarding the magnetic properties, the $X_{\mathrm{M}}T$ values for MnCr, FeCr, and CoCr frameworks increase gradually with cooling to cryogenic temperature and then steeply become large at $20~\mathrm{K}$ for MnCr, 8 K for FeCr, and $9.5\mathrm{~K~}$ for $\mathbf{CoCr}_{\mathrm{{r}}}$ indicating the onset of ferromagnetic ordering. The critical temperature $\left(T_{\mathrm{c}}\right)$ of three compounds obtained from the magnetization study was $5.5\mathrm{~K~}$ for MnCr. For FeCr and $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{C}}\mathbf{\boldsymbol{o}}\mathbf{\boldsymbol{C}}\mathbf{\mathbf{r}}$ frameworks, $T_{\mathrm{c}}$ was estimated to be 9.0 and $10.0\mathrm{K},$ respectively. The proton conductivity was measured by EIS at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in the RH range $(40-75\%)$ due to structural decomposition. As a control experiment, $\{\mathrm{N}(n{\cdot}\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{9})_{4}\}[\mathrm{Mn}\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]$ including the hydrophobic $\{\mathrm{N}(n{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{9})_{4}\}^{+}$ ion exhibits $1.1\times10^{-9}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ under $95\%$ RH. However, the hydrophilic $\{\mathrm{NH(prol)}_{3}\}[\mathrm{MCr}-$ $\left(\mathbf{ox}\right)_{3}]$ $\left(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}},\mathbf{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}},\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}^{\mathrm{II}}\right)$ have a high conductivity of ${\sim}1\times$ $10^{-4}\mathrm{{S}\ c m^{-1}}$ around $75\%$ RH. To obtain an insight for proton conductivity, the water vapor isotherm was measured before decomposition at relative pressure. Under the low humidity condition ( $(<40\%$ RH), two $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules are involved in $\mathbf{MnCr}$ and one $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ is included in FeCr and $\mathbf{\mathop{CoCr}}$ compounds. At high humidity, $\mathbf{MnCr}$ and FeCr have $n\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\big)=5$ and CoCr is 3.5 at $73\%$ RH. Therefore, three additional water molecules under $75-80\%$ RH are responsible for the high proton conductivity. This report is the first example of MOFs exhibiting the dual functionality of ferromagnetism and proton conduction.
In 2019, the synthesis of the rare oxalate MOF-type minerals called stepanovite (ST) and zhemchuzhnikovite (ZH) (discovered in the Lower Lena region of Siberia)107 was reported by Huskic et al. for proton conductivity.108 ST consists of Na and Fe ion species connected by oxalate for a 2D framework with $\mathbf{ABCA^{\prime}B^{\prime}C^{\prime}}$ stacking $(\sim37\ \mathring\mathrm{A})$ impregnating the $\mathrm{Mg\big(H_{2}O\big)}_{6}{}^{2+}$ cation in the cavity, $\big[\mathrm{Mg}\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\big)_{6}\big]\big[\mathrm{NaFe}\big(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\big)_{3}\big]\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ While
ZH has a partial substitution of the Fe ion with Al $\big[\mathrm{Mg}\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\big)_{6}\big]\bar{[\mathrm{NaFe}_{x}\mathrm{Al}_{1-x}\big(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\big)_{3}]}\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ $x\sim0.6)$ and AB stacking $(\sim12\mathrm{~\AA~})$ (Figure 9). The ST compound has polymorphs, ST1 and ST2, depending on the synthetic condition, where slow evaporation gives hexagonal plate-like crystals (ST1, thermodynamically preferred), whereas rapid evaporation produces elongated crystals (ST2, kinetically preferred) similar to ZH. The compounds differ in a stacking manner. The impedance measurements of ST1, ST2, and ZH reveal considerable enhancement as the temperature and humidity increase. At $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and $90\%$ RH, the conductivity of ZH is $3\dot{\times}10^{-3}\mathrm{Scm^{-1}}$ . However, the impedance of ST2 cannot be measured under high humidity due to deliquescence. Nevertheless, it shows the highest conductivity up to $70\%$ RH among the compounds. In the $E_{\mathrm{a}},$ ST2 reveals a higher value $(1.31~\mathrm{eV})$ than ST1 $(0.59\ \mathrm{eV})$ and ZH $(0.37~\mathrm{eV})$ because the measurement was performed under low humidity condition. Furthermore, the PXRD data of ST2 after impedance measurement shows a structural transformation to ST1 that would be part of the reason for the high $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ . Compared with other oxalate MOFs, it shows higher conductivity that can be attributed to the hexaaquo ligand in the countercation because the coordinate water is more acidic than free water molecules. To prove the species of conductive ion, isotope $\left(\mathrm{H}/\mathrm{D}\right)$ effect was measured with deuterated ST1 and ZH because these compounds include the counterion $\mathrm{(Mg(H_{2}O)_{6})^{2+}}$ . The conductivity of deuterated ST1 has a ca. 1.3-fold reduction from $1.76\times10^{-3}\mathrm{Scm^{-1}}$ to 1.40 $\times~10^{-3}~\mathrm{{S}~c m^{-1}}$ with a similar $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ value $(0.51~\mathrm{eV})$ . This work demonstrated the proton conductivity and the isotope effect with structure analysis in a 2D structured natural mineral.
For intrinsic proton conductivity in MOFs, carrier concentration, acidity, and structural factors are required in the framework itself. In 2012, Umeyama et al. studied the intrinsic anhydrous and anisotropic proton conductivity in a 2D Znbased 1,2,4-triazole $(\mathrm{TzH}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3})$ , and orthophosphate $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4})$ framework, $\mathrm{[Zn(H_{2}P O_{4})_{2}(T z H)_{2}]_{\it n},}$ through impedance measurement of the single and powder samples (Figure 10).45 In single-crystal X-ray analysis of $\mathrm{[Zn(H_{2}P O_{4})_{2}(T a H)_{2}]_{\it n},}$ Zn has octahedral coordination by two monocoordinated orthophosphates and four bridging TzHs resulting in a 2D layer (Figure 10). The layers are connected by the inter/intra Hbonding network of monocoordinated orthophosphate stacking along the C-direction. In addition, the TzH bridge provides an ideal interval of $4.6{-}4.8\mathring{\mathrm{A}},$ where the phosphonate achieves an acidic proton H-bond. This compound is thermally stable until $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and decomposes/melts from $180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The crosspolarization magic-angle-spinning (CP-MAS) SSNMR for $^{31}\mathrm{P}$ confirmed the purity of the sample, coordination number, and degree of condensation. The NMR spectrum supports the absence of free $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4}$ species and of diphosphoric and polyphosphoric acid. The ionic conductivity was obtained by alternating current (AC) impedance spectroscopy in the temperature range from 45 to $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under a ${\bf N}_{2}$ atmosphere.
The complex modulus plot shows two clear semicircles that are considered as the bulk phase and grain boundary (GB). At 45 ${}^{\circ}{\bf C},$ the conductivity in the pelletized sample is $1.4\times10^{-6}\mathrm{Scm^{-1}}$ for the bulk phase, whereas the GB shows $1.8\times10^{-7}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ . However, two distinct phases merged from $100\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ including the overall conductivity $\left(\mathbf{bulk}+\mathbf{GB}\right)$ . The conductivity becomes $1.2\times10^{-4}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ at $150\ {}^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ and $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ is $0.6~\mathrm{eV}$ assuming the Grotthuss mechanism due to the absence of mobile species. From the direct current (DC) conductivity, by manufacturing a membrane-electrode assembly (MEA), the open circuit voltage (OCV) is $0.65\mathrm{V}$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $0.50\ \mathrm{eV}$ at $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ respectively. The plausible conducting mechanism was demonstrated by solid-state $^2\mathrm{H}$ NMR analysis at 25 and $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The NMR data exhibit an isotropic signal and an anisotropic signal at both temperatures with a change in peak sharpness, which means that there are two types of deuterium species with high and low mobility. The isotropic signal is thought to be the rotation of the ${\mathrm{-PO}}_{3}$ group, and the anisotropic one can be the static deuteron from nitrogen in TzH.109 This result suggests that the protonconduction pathway is the AB-plane (2D layer). The conductivity of the deuterated sample is 2 orders of magnitude lower than that of the pristine one due to the sensitivity of the mass difference in quantum tunneling. With respect to the anisotropic conducting pathway, single-crystal conductivity was measured with a large single crystal $\dot{0.55}\times0.25\times0.06~\mathrm{mm}$ ). The in-plane (AB-plane direction) and out-of-plane ( $C_{-}$ direction) conductivities are $1.1\times10^{-4}$ and $2.9\ \mathrm{~\bar{\times}~}10^{-6}\ \mathrm{~S~}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively, where the in-plane value is similar to the bulk value and strongly supports the solid-state $^2\mathrm{H}$ NMR study. The relation and overlap of single crystal and modulus plot (Figure 10d) mean a negligible GB effect and long-range proton migration.
Xu and coworkers have achieved superprotonic conductivity in a highly oriented crystalline MOF nanofilm (Cu−TCPP; $\mathrm{H_{2}T C P P}~=~5,10,15,20.$ -tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)porphyrin; $\mathrm{C_{48}H_{30}N_{4}O_{8}})$ (Figure 11).110 The MOF nanofilm with a high aspect ratio ( $15\mathrm{nm}$ thick and $400\mathrm{nm}$ in diameter) was prepared by the modular assembly method.111 $\mathtt{C u}$ -TCPP has a 2D structure, where the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{(COO)}_{4}$ paddle wheels are connected by a TCPP ligand with an AB stacking mode. In addition, this film shows a highly oriented structure on the substrate. In grazing incidence XRD (GIXRD) analysis, in-plane and out-ofplane indicate only the $\left(h k0\right)$ and (00l) peak, respectively (Figure 11b). The stacking structure attains the porosity with the 1D channel of $1.1\ \mathrm{\nm}$ along the $c$ -axis resulting in a Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) surface area of $485~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The ionic conductivity was evaluated by the quasi-four-probe method using a ${350}\mathrm{nm}$ thick nanofilm located between the two electrodes (distance $=100\mu\mathrm{m}\cdot$ ). The proton conductivity is 3.9 $\times10^{-3}\mathrm{Scm^{-1}}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $98\%$ RH, which is comparable to the MOFs including acid guests. The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ for proton conduction is $0.28\ \mathrm{eV}.$ . For further study of exceptional proton-conducting performance, in situ GIXRD was measured under humidity control (Figure 11b). However, there was no difference in the diffraction pattern with and without humidity, and this result implies that structure transformation by the gate opening phenomenon can be excluded in this 2D MOF. Nevertheless, water vapor sorption isotherm represents capillary condensation at $P/P_{0}>0.9$ with a steep increase of adsorption capacity though negligible adsorption amounts at low pressure.
Typically, pore-filling with a pore size of $1.1\mathrm{nm}$ is observed at $P/P_{0}=0.5$ , whereas capillary condensation at a relatively higher pressure is derived from mesopore or macropore filling between nanosheets. In particular, the analysis of the correlation between $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorption amounts and conductivity proposes that an efficient conducting pathway is dominantly achieved with surface water at low pressure (inset of Figure 11c). Therefore, the high conductivity is attributed to the surface functional groups in a 2D layer such as the terminal carboxyl group of a ligand and coordinated $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ molecules on the axial site of the Cu paddle wheel. This study clearly demonstrated that highly oriented packing of the 2D nanosheet with dangling functional groups on the surface has a crucial role for high proton conductivity in nanoscale MOF crystals.
## 4. DESIGN AND STRATEGIES FOR IMPROVING PROTON CONDUCTIVITY IN METAL−ORGANIC FRAMEWORKS
To date, proton-conductive MOFs have been achieved by the various design strategies and approaches to improve carrier concentration with acidity and high mobility with an efficient conducting pathway. In this section, we discuss representative examples of proton-conductive MOFs through detailed classification.
## 4.1. Counterions in a Charged Network
Most MOFs have a neutral framework by connection of metal cations and anionic organic linkers. Nonetheless, some MOFs are accidentally synthesized or predesigned as a charged network during the synthesizing procedure through the judicious choice of organic solvents, metal ions, and organic ligands. In this case, MOFs impregnate either cation or anion species as counterions in the void space for an overall charge balance with inherent strong electrostatic interactions. Typically, the charged MOFs are classified into cationic and anionic MOFs, where the positively or negatively charged character means the skeleton and counterions act as an extra framework. For example, in an anionic framework, amide-type solvents like $N,N.$ -dimethylformamide (DMF), $N,N.$ -dimethylacetamide (DMA), and $_{N,N}.$ diethylformamide (DEF) can be decomposed into $\mathrm{[NH}_{2}\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{]}^{+}$ or $[\mathrm{\dot{N}H_{2}E t_{2}}]^{+}$ cations by hydrolysis under solvothermal conditions and play the counterions.112 The counterions can be exchanged with functional guest ions for promising applications such as ion exchange materials, selective gas storage/separation, and catalyst.113−117 In particular, protonic species such as $\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $\mathbf{Me_{2}}^{+}$ , and $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}^{+}$ in anionic frameworks can participate in proton diffusion through proton donation or molecular diffusion motion with an efficient Hbonding. In 2014, Sadakiyo and coworkers demonstrated the cation effect in proton conductivity with H-bond control by $\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}$ cation substitution in $(\mathrm{NH_{4}})_{2}(\mathrm{adp})[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ with $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ .118 The countercations should have a major contribution to the proton transfer as well as conduction pathways. In this study, the authors directly synthesized isostructural $(\mathrm{NH}_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{adp})[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\operatorname{K}_{2}(\mathsf{a d p})$ - $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ without a change in structure and guest arrangement, as shown by the single-crystal X-ray analysis (Figure 12). However, the H-bond network between the cations and guest molecules has a significant difference due to the inclusion of nonhydrogen bonding metal ion species. Although most of the atom positions and distances are the same in both compounds, there is missing H-bonding around the $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ . This is indicative of a Coulomb interaction between water guests and the $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ ion rather than an H-bond interaction. This difference clearly affects proton conductivity. In the anhydrous condition, both compounds represent a similar conductivity value $(2.2\times$ $10^{-12}\mathrm{Scm^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{K}_{2}\bar{(\mathrm{adp)}}[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]$ and $1.4\times10^{-12}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for $(\mathrm{NH}_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{adp})[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ implying no cation migration.
Under the high-humidity condition $98\%$ RH), the compound $\mathrm{K}_{2}(\mathrm{adp})[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ has a conductivity of 1.2 $\times10^{-4}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ which is 2 orders of magnitude lower than that of $(\mathrm{NH}_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{adp})[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(0.8~\times~10^{-2}~\leq$ S $\mathsf{c m}^{-1},$ ). The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ of $\mathrm{K}_{2}(\mathrm{adp})[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ from the Arrhenius plot is $0.45\mathrm{eV.}$ . In fact, the ammonium cation contributes to the proton conductivity in two manners in this system. One is the increase in carrier concentration because of the acidic character and another is for the conducting pathway with the formation of an H-bonding network. However, the potassium ion shows the absence of these roles. Therefore, the conductivity decrease is reasonable. A further study of neutron scattering for both materials emphasized that rotational molecular motion of ammonium ions is a crucial factor for high proton conductivity (see section 6.3). These results describe well the manner of conductivity control in MOFs and provide a fundamental method to promote proton conductivity.
In 2011, Pardo et al. reported high proton conductivity in 3D chiral ferromagnetic anionic MOF, $(\mathrm{NH}_{4})_{4}[\mathrm{MnCr}_{2}\dot{(}\mathrm{ox})_{6}]_{3}$ $\ensuremath{4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ .119 The oxalate-based bimetallic $\mathrm{(Cr,~Mn)}$ compound incorporates ammonium ions and water molecules in the functionalized channel. There are two types of channel with different sizes of 5.23 and 7.52 Å for A and B, respectively (Figure 13). In particular, the terminal of the oxalate ligand of $\bar{\mathrm{[Cr(ox)_{3}]^{3-}}}$ directs to the center of pore A to stabilize the water molecules. Even though pore B is also a good environment for embedding water molecules, it is difficult to refine the position due to the disorder. The ammoniums are located around the dangle oxalate ligand in channel A for charge compensation. The ammonium and the terminal oxalate have H-bonds with the distance of $2.795(6){-}2.960(8)\mathrm{\AA}.$ These H-bonds continuously connect with the central water channel with a distance of $2.745\bigl(13\bigr)-2.888\bigl(16\bigr)\mathring{\mathrm{A}}.$ . The other organic ligand connects the Mn (II) and $\mathrm{Cr}$ (III) in a bis-bidentate way. The conductivity estimated from impedance measurement was $1.1\times10^{-3}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-\mathrm{i}}$ at $295\mathrm{K}$ and $96\%$ RH and increased to $1.7\times10^{-3}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at 313 $\mathrm{K,}$ exhibiting the Grotthuss mechanism ( $\dot{\mathrm{~\cal~E~}}_{\mathrm{a}}$ of $0.23\ \mathrm{eV},$ ). This charged chiral compound shows not only an outstanding proton-conducting property but also a conducting pathway through the single-crystal method.
The hydrazinium lanthanide-oxalate ${\bf\cal M O F s}_{,\ }$ , $(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5})[\mathrm{CeEu-}$ $(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4})_{4}(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5})]{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\left(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}\right)\left[\mathrm{Nd}_{2}\big(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}\big)_{4}\big(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}\big)\right]$ · $\mathrm{4H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\cdot}$ , are studied for proton conductivity and luminescence emission by Zhang et al. in 2017.120 The lanthanide MOFs include the hydrazinium counterion with a higher water stability than the typical oxalate MOFs. In the structure of $(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5})$ - $\big[\mathrm{CeEu}\big(\mathrm{C}_{2}\dot{\mathrm{O}_{4}}\big)_{4}\big(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}\big)\big]{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ the oxalate ligand shows two different coordination modes: $\mu_{3}$ -bridge mode and $\mu_{2}$ -bridge mode.
Concomitantly, the hexagonal honeycomb monolayer is extended along the AB-plane and two monolayers are connected by $\mathrm{LnO}_{9}$ polyhedra. This compound displays an identical PXRD pattern and morphology after immersion in water for 15 days. To check the proton conductivity, AC impedance was measured at $298{-}313\mathrm{~K~}$ and $100\%$ RH in the frequency region of $10^{2}$ to ${\bf{10^{6}H z}}$ . The conductivity at $298\mathrm{K}$ and $100\%$ RH is $\phantom{+}3.42\times10^{-3}S$ $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ , and improves up to $4.80\times10^{-3}\mathrm{{Scm}^{-1}}$ at 313 K with an $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ of $0.10\ \mathrm{eV}$ . Although the proton-conductive MOFs have been widely studied for the past decade, a few of the lanthanide MOFs show high RT proton conductivity. The hydration of lanthanide MOFs affects not only proton conductivity but also the reduction in luminescence emission. ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ has a characteristic luminescence emission at 582, 618, and $692~\mathrm{nm}$ derived from ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}$ to $\mathrm{{}^{7}F_{1},\mathrm{{}^{5}D_{0}}}$ to $^7\mathrm{F}_{2},$ and ${}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}$ to $\mathrm{{}^{7}F_{4}}$ transitions. Typically, the luminescence intensity of the lanthanide ion in the coordination compound is uninhibited by the coordination environment due to the shielding of the $4\mathrm{f}{-}4\mathrm{f}$ transition by 5s and 5p orbitals. Therefore, the reduced luminescence intensity is thought to be an energy transfer from ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ to $-\mathrm{OH}$ in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . The linear correlation of proton conductivity increases and the luminescence decreases as the humidity increase entirely expresses an electrical signal to an optical signal. This concept is confirmed in another lanthanide compound, $\mathrm{(N_{2}H_{5})[N d_{2}(C_{2}O_{4})_{4}(N_{2}H_{5})]}$ · $\mathrm{4H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\cdot}$ , with the other working wavelength (NIR).
Tu et al. reported a high proton conductivity in a mixed ligand MOF of $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}\big(\mathrm{BDC}\big)_{2}(\mathrm{NDC})(\mathrm{SO}_{4})_{4}(\mathrm{DMA})_{4}$ [VNU-15; $\mathrm{\DeltaVNU=}$
Vietnam National University; $\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{II}}/F e^{\mathrm{III}}}$ ; ${\mathrm{BDC}}=$ benzene-1,4- dicarboxylate; ${\mathrm{NDC}}=$ naphthalene-2,6-dicarboxylate; $\mathrm{DMA}=$ dimethylammonium] under the low humidity condition.121 VNU-15 was prepared by heating the mixture of $\mathrm{FeSO}_{4},\mathrm{CuCl}_{2},$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC},$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{NDC}$ in DMF for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ . In the reaction, $\mathrm{CuCl}_{2}$ and 9,10-anthraquinone serve as redox agents leading to DMA. Without both, VNU-15 is not formed. The metal ions in secondary building units (SBUs) $[\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{3}(\mathrm{SO}_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{DMA})_{2}]$ are bridged by ${\bar{\mathrm{SO}_{4}}}^{2-}$ formed chain, and then both ligands 1 $\mathrm{BDC}^{2-}$ and $\mathrm{\boldmath~\NDC}^{2-}$ ) interconnect the metal chains with one another (Figure 14). The DMA counterions are located around sulfate ligand with H-bonds (a distance of $\mathrm{N{\mathrm{-}H\mathrm{\cdots}O\mathrm{-}S}}$ is $1.90-$ $1.96\mathring\mathrm{A}\big)$ . The successive H-bond along the metal chain proposes the potential proton-conducting property. At $30\%$ RH and 95 ${}^{\circ}{\bf C},$ the pelletized VNU-15 exhibits the conductivity of $2.38\times$ $10^{-4}\mathrm{{S}\thinspace\dot{~}\dot{\mathrm{{cm}}}^{-1}}$ and increases up to $2.9\times10^{-2}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at $60\%$ RH. This conductivity is 2.5 times higher than that of Nafion at similar conditions $\left(1.0\times10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}\right.$ at $60\%$ RH and $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\ '$ ). The calculated $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ from the Arrhenius plot implies the Grotthuss mechanism with 0.24 and $0.22\ \mathrm{~eV}$ at 55 and $60\%$ RH, respectively. It concludes that the H-bond between the bridge sulfate in SBUs and DMA cation is a key factor for the high conductivity in anionic Fe-framework.
In 2017, the unique proton dynamics was studied in a charged Eu framework, $(\mathrm{N}\bar{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\bar{\mathrm{M}}\mathbf{e}_{2})[\mathrm{Eu}(\mathrm{L}).$ ; $\mathrm{C_{11}H_{13}N O_{7}E u P}]$ $(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}=5\cdot$ - (phosphonomethyl)isophthalic acid), by Wei and coworkers.47 This compound is constructed by an anionic $[\mathrm{Eu(L)}]^{-}2\mathrm{D}$ wavy layered architecture and intercalation of the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{Me}_{2}^{+}$ counterion between the layers. The 2D layer is extended along the BCplane and the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{Me}_{2}^{+}$ ion has a strong H-bond interaction with the uncoordinated O atom of the phosphonate group in the ligand forming the $\mathrm{N-H\cdotsO}$ chain along the $c$ -axis. Their Hbond is characterized by two broad peaks in infrared (IR)
spectrum (2000−2550 and $2550{-}2800~\mathrm{cm^{-1}},$ ), corresponding to two different distances between the donor (N) and acceptor (O) in single-crystal X-ray analysis. The proton conductivity is evaluated using a single crystal and a pellet under anhydrous and water-assisted conditions. The single-crystal conductivity under the anhydrous condition shows anisotropic proton conductivity along the $c$ -axis $(1.25\times10^{-3}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $E_{\mathrm{a}}=0.21\ \mathrm{eV},$ ), whereas there is no conductivity in other directions. In the same condition, pelletized sample indicates ${\sim}10^{-7}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ due to the large GB effect where the $_{\mathrm{N-H-O}}$ chain is not effectively connected between the microcrystals. Under $98\%$ RH and $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the pelletized sample has the highest conductivity of $3.76\times10^{-3}\mathrm{S{\cdot}c m^{-1}}$ with $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{0.72eV}}$ because the inserted water molecules can facilitate proton diffusion, establishing a bridge for the proton-conducting pathway. The OCV at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $98\%$ RH is $0.87\mathrm{~V},$ comparable to the previous report.122 To understand the efficient proton conductivity, in situ singlecrystal X-ray analysis with temperature control was performed and revealed an anisotropic thermal expansion. In particular, the severe vibrational dynamic motion is observed in the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{Me}_{2}^{+}$ cation. The electron-density distribution map of the hydrogenbonded H atoms shows that one of the protons in the counterion gradually escapes from the N atom as temperature rises, whereas another proton keeps its bond length. It is reversible during the temperature sweeping. The in situ diffuse reflectance infrared Fourier transform spectrum (DRIFTS) at VT supports the single-crystal $\mathrm{x}$ -ray analysis with a red-shift peak caused by elongation of the $\mathrm{\DeltaN-H}$ bond (Figure 15c). This implies that a sufficient thermal supply can promote the dissociation of the proton from $\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{Me}_{2}^{+}$ . Although ${\bf M e}_{2}{\bf N H}$ has a low bp of 7.4 ${}^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ it is released from the framework at ${\sim}350{\mathrm{-}}450\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ due to deprotonation in the confined environment showing a new phase $\left[\mathrm{Eu(HL)}\right]$ in PXRD with the disappearance of two strong H-bonding signals in IR. However, the appearance of a peak at $3280~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ in IR for a new phase means $\mathrm{PO-H}$ (acidification of ${\mathrm{PO}}_{3})$ , how a proton moves in the framework. The $\left[\mathrm{Eu(HL)}\right]$ phase was confirmed by single-crystal analysis. Comparing the conductivity of $\left[\mathrm{Eu(HL)}\right]$ with $(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{Me}_{2})[\mathrm{Eu(L)}]$ at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $98\%$ RH, $\left[\mathrm{Eu(HL)}\right]$ has conductivity 2 orders of magnitude lower $(5.29~\times~10^{-5}~\mathrm{~S~}\mathrm{~cm}^{-1}$ , $E_{\mathrm{a}}~=~1.50~\mathrm{eV})$ than that of $(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{Me}_{2})[\mathrm{Eu(L)}]$ $\cdot3.76\times10^{-3}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $E_{\mathrm{a}}=0.38~\mathrm{eV},$ , which means that the strong H-bond by counterion promotes proton conductivity. The irreversible conductivity corresponds to the irreversible photoluminescence signal in the temperaturedependent luminescence measurement; nevertheless, the heattreatment of $\left[\mathrm{Eu(HL)}\right]$ in a ${\mathrm{Me}}_{2}{\mathrm{NH}}$ aqueous solution recovers the H-bond chain.
