A steep conductivity increase occurs in the temperature range of $45\mathrm{-}55\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ where a structure change is observed through the variable-temperature PXRD patterns. This is thought to be a fluctuation of the guest molecule arrangement. Therefore, the $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ was evaluated at two temperature ranges with a best linear fit of $0.17\ \mathrm{eV}$ for the lower temperature range $(15-45^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ and 0.10 $\mathrm{eV}$ for the high temperature range $(55-65~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , respectively. The low $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ suggests that the Grotthuss mechanism is a dominant contribution in proton diffusion in this charged material.
To date, the number of charged MOFs reported with excellent performance is increasing. Although the charged frameworks are promising for high proton-conducting materials, their synthesis and stability are still challenging, with few examples. In addition, the undefined position of counterions still makes conducting pathways ambiguous. Therefore, future endeavors to produce new charge materials with high structural stability and a comprehensive conducting pathway are urgently required.
## 4.2. Ligand and Metal Center Functionalization
The tunability of metal centers and organic linkers is one of the major advantages that MOF materials have for property improvement. Moreover, the introduction of various functional groups alters the properties of the surface and pore environment in MOFs, resulting in a change in physical properties. To functionalize the frameworks, the predesigned synthesis and postsynthetic modification methods are used with a judicious design strategy. For the predesign method, the selection of metal or organic species for additional functional groups and metal clusters is critical. By contrast, postsynthetic modifications on the metal center or organic linker are relatively simple and have broad applications in various materials without structural change.52,54,127,128 In particular, the incorporation of acidic functional groups is beneficial for proton-conductive MOFs to enhance their carrier concentration. In addition, other functional groups such as amine and hydroxy can obtain the protonhopping site through the H-bond network (Figure 18).
In 2011, Shigematsu and coworkers have studied various functional group effects in proton conductivity with MIL-53 derivatives (MIL-53(M)-(BDC-R), (here, $\mathbf{M}\overset{\cdot}{=}\mathbf{A}\mathbf{l}^{3+}$ or $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ; $\mathrm{BDC}~=~1,4$ -benzenedicarboxylate $(\mathrm{C}_{8}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{4})$ ; $\mathrm{~R~}=\mathrm{~-H~}$ (1), $\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ (2), −OH (3), or COOH (4)) (Figure 19).50 MIL-53 derivatives are well-known for isostructure, chemical stability, and large structure flexibility with a 1D channel.129 The hydroxo ligand $\scriptstyle(\mu\mathrm{-OH})$ bridges all-metal units forming a 1D chain, and isophthalic acid interlinks the metal chain, resulting in 3D structure. The additional functional groups in an organic linker direct toward the channel that is able to interact with guest molecules. The various functional groups induce a different $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ (4.74 for $\ensuremath{-\mathrm{NH}_{2}},$ 4.19 for $-\mathrm{H},\ 4.08$ for $-\mathrm{OH},$ and 3.62 for $-\mathrm{COOH},$ ),130,131 which is correlated with proton conductivity. The proton conductivities of four compounds at $298\mathrm{K}$ and $95\%$
RH are $2.3\times10^{-8}$ $\mathrm{(-H)}$ , $2.3\times10^{-9}$ $\left(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\right)$ , $4.2\times10^{-7}$ $\scriptstyle(-\mathrm{OH})$ , and $2.0\times10^{-6}\mathrm{Scm^{-1}}$ $\scriptstyle(-\mathrm{COOH})$ , respectively. The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ values from temperature-dependent conductivity are 0.47, 0.45, 0.27, and $0.21\ \mathrm{eV}_{i}$ respectively. The $\scriptstyle\mu\mathrm{-OH}$ and the coordinated carboxylate group which are the same components in MIL-53 derivatives, are fundamental proton carriers and are independent of acidity. Therefore, the acidity of materials is fully controlled by additional functional groups. The order of acidity demonstrates a good correlation with proton conductivity (acidity and conductivity: $-\mathrm{NH}_{2}<-\mathrm{H}<-\mathrm{OH}<-\mathrm{COOH}$ and $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ : $\mathrm{-NH}_{2},$ $-\mathrm{H}>-\mathrm{OH}>-\mathrm{COOH})$ .
Although these isostructural compounds show different water uptake amounts, (equivalent per metal unit in 1 $\mathrm{(-H)}$ , 1 $(\mathrm{\bar{-}N H}_{2}),5(\mathrm{\bar{-}O H}).$ , and 1.5 $\scriptstyle\left(-\mathbf{COOH}\right)$ ), it is thought to be the structural flexibility. Comparing $-\mathrm{OH}$ and −COOH, both compounds show high $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ uptake amounts and relatively high proton conductivity, whereas the dominant contribution is the acidity from the functional group due to the excess water that is not effective to enhance the conductivity in $\scriptstyle(-\mathrm{OH})$ . Consequently, $\mathrm{MIL}{-}53(\mathrm{Fe}){-}\mathrm{COOH}$ has the highest proton conductivity with the lowest $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ due to the lowest $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ . In 2014, Yamada and coworkers studied fine-tuning of water adsorption and proton conductivity by controlling the fractional functional group ratio in the mixed ligand system of Al(OH)(BDC$\mathsf{\bar{O}H}\bar{\mathsf{\Sigma}}_{x}(\mathrm{BDC}\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{1-x}$ .132 In a previous report,50 the MIL${53}(\mathrm{Al})({-}\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ and $\mathrm{MIL}{-}53\mathrm{(Al)}({-}\mathrm{OH})$ compounds display distinct $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ adsorption amounts at $298\mathrm{K}$ However, the mixed ligand system in the crystal with different ratios affects not only the gate opening pressure but also the total uptake amounts, which strongly affect proton conductivity in these compounds. In fact, because of the isostructural property (single patternnarrow pore (NP)), it is difficult to distinguish the mixed ligand system involving different ligand ratios in the PXRD pattern from $\mathrm{MIL}{-}53(\bar{\mathrm{Al}})({-}\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ or $\mathrm{MIL-}53(\mathrm{Al})(-\mathrm{OH})$ . However, the difference in flexibility induced by different ratios of ${-\mathrm{NH}_{2}/}$ $-\mathrm{OH}$ functional group represent two types of diffraction patterns ( $_x>0.5=$ large pore (LP) and $x<0.5=$ narrow pore (NP)) under the humidified condition. In water sorption, a breathing effect is observed as the BDC-OH ligand ratio increases with improved water uptake amounts. The proton conductivity of $\mathrm{\hat{M}I L}{-}53(\mathrm{Al})(-\mathrm{O\hat{H}})$ is $\sim10^{-6}~\mathrm{~S~}~\mathrm{cm}^{\hat{-}1}$ and $\left(\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\right)$ is 3 orders of magnitude lower at $298\mathrm{K}$ and $95\%$ RH.50 At the low humidity condition ( $40\%$ RH), there is no correlation to the ligand ratio in the mixed ligand system, whereas BDC-OH is effective at the high-humidity condition $95\%$ RH) with proportional conductivity enhancement. Although the absolute conductivity value is not high, the fine-tuning of conductivity via functional group adjustment is interesting. Fundamentally, the acid functional group in MOFs naturally donates protons to the guest $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ through the hydration process, generating the $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}^{+}{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ conjugated acid−base system in the MOF channel. In 2019, Lim et al. achieved an analogous system by MIL-53(Al) derivatives using anhydrous $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ as a conducting medium.42 To render an $\mathrm{NH_{4}^{+}{-}N H_{3}}$ conjugated acid−base system in MOFs, $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ gas sorption is carried out in $\mathrm{MIL}{-}53(\mathrm{Al}){-}(\mathrm{COOH})_{2},$ where additional $-\mathrm{COOH}$ functional groups provide a proton to $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ for $\mathrm{NH_{4}^{+}}$ and sequential $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ adsorption completes the $\mathrm{NH_{4}^{+}{-}N H_{3}}$ chain in the pore for the Grotthuss-type protonconduction mechanism. Nevertheless, the conductivity value is 1 order of magnitude lower than that of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ -mediated conductivity due to the lower ionization character of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ . This study demonstrates that the exact uncoordinated functional group control can improve proton conductivity in MOFs.
In 2012, Banerjee group reported the proton conductivity of triazole-functionalized In or Cd-based-metal−organic nanotube (MONTs) (Figure 20).126 To construct MONTs, they used $\operatorname{In}(\operatorname{III})$ and $\operatorname{Cd}\bar{(\operatorname{II})}$ metal salt with 5-triazole isophthalic acid (5- TIA) resulting in In-5TIA $\big[\mathrm{In}\big(\mathrm{C}_{10}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{N}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{5}\big)_{2}$ - $(\mathrm{\bar{C}}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{8}\mathrm{N})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]$ and Cd-5TIA $\left[\mathrm{Cd}\big(\mathrm{C}_{10}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{N}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{5}\big)_{2}\big(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{8}\mathrm{N}\big)_{2}\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\big)\right]$ . Both compounds incorporating counterions have a 1D single wall tube along the c-axis with an inner diameter of 7.85 and $8.23\mathrm{~\AA},$ respectively, whereas In-IA without triazole functional group has an inner diameter of $4.8\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}.$ The tubes are connected by Hbonding $\left(\mathrm{C-H\cdotsO}\right)$ and the triazole functional groups are located on the outer surface of NTs. In proton-conducting property at $301~\mathrm{K}$ and $98\%$ RH, In-IA, In-5TIA, and Cd-5TIA show proton conductivity of $2.2\times10^{-4}$ $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}=0.47~\mathrm{eV}\right)$ , $5.35\times$ $10^{-5}$ ( $\left[0.137~\mathrm{eV}\right)$ , $3.61\times\dot{1}0^{-3}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ $\left(0.163\ \mathrm{eV}\right)$ , respectively. The difference in conductivity value between the In and Cd compounds is attributed to the different numbers of $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+}$ countercations in the frameworks. The presence of the triazole functional group facilitates proton diffusion in the frameworks reflecting a low $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ .
In contrast to the predesigned ligand functionalization, the introduction of a functional group in the ligand through the postsynthetic modification after MOF synthesis is interesting.
The highly decorated surface of three PCPs with the sulfonic acid group were reported by Ramaswamy in 2014.133 To prepare the three compounds, a solvothermal reaction was performed using $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ with 5-sulfo-isophthalic acid $\left(5\mathrm{-}\mathrm{sipH}_{3}\right)$ ) and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine (bpy) in $N,N^{\prime}$ - dimethylformamide (DMF) at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ (Figure 21), affording Compound 1, $[Z\mathbf{n}(5\cdot$ sipH)- (bpy)] ${\cdot}\mathrm{DMF}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ Compound 2, $\left[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\right.$ (5-sipH)- $\big(\mathrm{bpe}\big)_{0.5}\big]{\cdot}\mathrm{DMF}_{.}$ , using 1,2-di(4-pyridyl)ethylene (bpe) instead of bpy, and Compound 3, $\mathrm{\small{[Zn_{3}(5-s i p)_{2}(5-s i p H)(b p y)]\cdot(D M F}},$ · 2(DMA) $\mathrm{\Delta\left[DMA=\right]}$ dimethylammonium, $\big(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\big)_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big\},$ ). Compound 1 is a 2D structure, and the layer stacking generates a 1D channel along the $a$ -axis with $5.6\times\dot{7}.1~i$ Å. Compound 2 is a 1D chain structure connected by bpe linkers exhibiting a ladder chain structure. The ladder chains are stacked each other providing a 1D channel along the $b$ -axis with $10.4\times3.1$ Å. Only compound 3 indicates a 3D structure with an octahedrally coordinated $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ center with a small pore of $3.4\times4.2\mathrm{{\AA}}.$ In this structure, two of three sulfonics are coordinated to the metal center, and the remaining one is protonated. In addition, two DMA cations and a DMF solvent are present in the pore. All compounds have a well-aligned proton source of 5-sip on the pore surface. Compound 1 shows a conductivity of $3.9\times10^{-4}S$ $\bar{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and $60\%$ RH, and compound 2 has a low conductivity of about $10^{-8}\mathrm{{Scm}^{-1}}$ at the same condition due to the absence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ guests. Compound 3 has a two-proton source of an ammonium cation and a sulfonic acid group, which imply the potential of an efficient proton conductor. Nevertheless, its conductivity is $8.7\times10^{-5}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ . It seems that the narrow channel of compound 3 does not favor proton diffusion, leading to low conductivity.
The partially coordinated sulfonate and phosphonate groups can serve as a proton-conduction pathway. In 2014, BazagaGarci ́a and coworkers reported on a calcium phosphonate framework (Ca-PiPhtA, (PiPhtA $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ (dihydroxyphosphoryl) isophthalic acid) treated with aqueous $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ for high proton conductivity.134 The pristine $C\mathsf{a}$ -PiPhtA-I shows a hydrophilic 1D channel along the $b$ -axis where the pore surface was decorated by partially coordinated carboxylate and phosphonate with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ coordinated in the metal center (two) and lattice water (five $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ per unit cell). A long-range order of the H-bond network is observed between the lattice $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules along the channel direction, and the removal of lattice water provides a partially dehydrated Ca-PiPhtA-II phase. The exposure of $\mathrm{Ca}.$ PiPhtA-I to $28\%$ aqueous $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ solution results in the Ca$\mathrm{PiPhtA}{\cdot}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{3}$ phase involving 7 $\cdot_{\mathrm{\DeltaNH}_{3}}$ and $16~\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules, whereas the dehydrated sample does not adsorb pure $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ in an $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ TPD measurement, which means cooperative ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}{\mathrm{-H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorption. The single-crystal structure of Ca-PiPhtA- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{3}$ is undetermined due to the insufficient crystallinity with partial decomposition. However, the strong H-bond in Ca-PiPhtA$\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ is verified by the red-shifted H-bond peaks in the IR spectrum. Without $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ treatment, the $C\mathsf{a}$ -PiPhtA-I and CaPiPhtA-II have conductivity values of $5.7\times10^{-4}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ $\left\langle E_{\mathrm{a}}=\right.$ $0.32\ \mathrm{{\eV})}$ and $3.6\times10^{-4}\mathrm{~S~}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at $24~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $98\%$ RH, respectively. At $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the conductivity increases to $1.3\times10^{-3}S$ $\mathsf{c m}^{\star-1}$ $\mathrm{'}E_{\mathrm{a}}=0.23\mathrm{eV}_{\mathrm{;}}$ Ca-PiPhtA-III). However, further increase in temperature reduced the conductivity due to the release of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules. The aqueous $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ treatment $\left(\mathrm{Ca-PiPhtA-NH_{3}}\right)$ ) indicated the highest conductivity $\left(6.6\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}\right)$ among these compounds at $24~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $98\%$ RH with $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ of $0.4~\mathrm{eV}$ . Although aqueous $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ promotes proton conductivity, there is no direct evidence for understanding their conducting pathway. Ramaswamy et al., investigated an $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ carboxyphosphonate framework (PCMOF10, $\mathrm{M}\bar{\bf g_{2}}(\mathrm{H}_{2}{\bf O})_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{L}){\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}{\bf O}$ $\mathrm{(H_{6}L}=2,5\mathrm{.}$ - dicarboxy-1,4-benzene-diphosphonic acid)) with a monoprotonated phosphonate group for extremely high water stability and proton conductivity.135 In PCMOF10, an edge-shared zigzag ladder-shaped $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ metal chain is connected by an organic linker forming a 2D layered architecture, which is extended to a BCplane and stacked along the $a$ -axis with a distance of 8.753(4) Å. The $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}$ metal has an octahedral coordination geometry, two oxygen atoms from $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\cdot}$ , one from carboxylate, and three from phosphonate. In particular, the phosphonate group is in the monoprotonated state, which protrudes into coordinated $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and carboxylate oxygen, resulting in a strong H-bond network. The excellent water stability of PCMOF10 (measured after soaking in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ for a week) shows a PXRD pattern corresponding to the original pattern. A high water stability and sufficient H-bond networks are reflected in the high proton conductivity of $3.55\times10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}=0.4~\mathrm{eV}\right)$ at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $95\%$ RH, which is reproducible in the temperature cycling process. This high conductivity is clearly humidity dependent; it decreased up to $7.2\times10^{-8}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ at $40\%$ RT and $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . The proton diffusion pathway in PCMOF10 contains lattice $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and coordinated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ with oxygen atoms from hydrogen phosphonate and carboxylate. In 2015, Phang and coworkers converted the thiol functional group in $\mathrm{UiO}{-}6\bar{6}({-}\mathrm{SH})$ into the sulfonic acid group (UiO${\bf\bar{66(-S O_{3}H)}},$ ) for increase of charge carrier density (Figure 22).
The sulfonic acid group is the strongest proton donor in which the $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ present in the pore easily receive protons and turn into $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}^{+}$ . To obtain the $-S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H},$ the oxidation reaction was performed with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ under the hydrothermal condition and the oxidation state of $(\mathrm{S}2\mathrm{p}_{3/2})$ is confirmed by a blue shift of binding energy (BE) in XPS. The extraordinary structure stability of $\mathrm{UiO}–66(\mathrm{-}\mathrm{SH})$ is beneficial for harsh reaction conditions. After the postsynthetic reaction, it still shows porosity with the decreased surface area due to the bulky functional group. Interestingly, the water vapor sorption isotherm of UiO$66(-\mathrm{{SO}_{3}\bar{H})}$ rapidly increases at the low-pressure region due to the high water affinity. The increased hydrophilicity helps to improve the proton conductivity. The thiol functional group in $\mathrm{Ui\bar{O}-}66(\mathrm{-}\mathrm{S\bar{H}})$ has a similar conductivity $(2.5\times10^{-5}\ \mathrm{{S}\ c m^{-1})}$ with the pristine UiO-66 $(4.3\times10^{-6}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $90\%$ RH. Meanwhile, UiO-66- $(\mathrm{{\calSO}}_{3}\mathrm{{\calH}})_{2}$ has a higher conductivity of $8.4\times10^{-2}\mathrm{Scm^{-1}}$ than the others under the same condition. It is mainly attributed to the existence of the Brønsted acid sites. The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ estimated from fitting of Arrhenius plot is $0.43\mathrm{eV}$ for UiO-66, $0.23\mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\big(\mathrm{SH}\big)_{2},$ and $0.32\mathrm{eV}$ for UiO-66- $\left(\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\right)_{2}$ . It suggests that the decorating of interior space by functional group facilitates proton diffusion in the framework. Although in the functionalized MOFs the Grotthus mechanism is expected from the $E_{\mathrm{a}},$ there is no direct evidence for the proton conduction pathway because of the difficulty of determining the position of conducting media and functional groups through the structural analysis.
Another important structural part of MOFs is the metal center. In MOF materials, the metal node is constructed by a single metal ion or a metal ion cluster. Depending on the metal species and synthesis conditions, metal centers have a coordinating solvent such as DMF, DEF, EtOH, MeOH, and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\cdot}$ , which can be removed by a thermal and a vacuum activation process providing open metal sites (OMSs). Therefore, the OMSs act as one of the strong interaction sites (Lewis acid sites) with guest molecules in the framework.136−139 In addition, the coordinative insertion of functional molecules into the OMSs is widely studied in the MOF field for applications of interest.140−142 In 2011, Jeong et al., reported the control of proton conductivity through the coordinative insertion of protic and aprotic molecules in a Cu paddle wheel of HKUST$1[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}]$ (Figure 23).52 The HKUST-1 is one of the representative MOF materials with OMSs after removal of coordinated solvent.143 The OMSs are located on the axial site of the $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ paddle wheel and the OMS modulation is carried out through the coordination chemistry with methanol (MeOHHK), ethanol (EtOH-HK), acetonitrile (MeCN-HK), and water 1 $_{\mathrm{{H_{2}O-H K}}})$ . Polar protic solvents such as MeOH, EtOH, and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ achieve the H-bond network with guest MeOH molecules for improved conductivity. Furthermore, among protic solvent, coordinated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ is the most effective for proton conduction $\left(1.5\times10^{-5}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\right)$ due to the sufficient acidity of $\mathrm{M}{-}^{+}\mathrm{OH}_{2}$ to donate a proton. As we can expect, a poor proton donor molecule like MeCN is not effective for proton conductivity at all. To understand the interaction between coordination molecules and guest molecules, the conductivity under MeOH vapor was compared with that under $n$ -hexane vapor. The $n$ - hexane is nonpolar and unable to form H-bonds, and shows low proton conductivity even in coordinated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . In fact, although the absolute conductivity values of these results are not high, this study provides a meaningful and potential strategy for using coordinative functional molecules insertion for proton-conductive MOFs.
In 2017, Zhang and coworkers studied how different arrangements of Im affect the overall proton conductivity in Fe-based MOFs (Figure 24).53 The Im molecules have been frequently incorporated in MOFs as a conducting medium for anhydrous or hydrate proton conductivity studies.144,145 To classify the Im arrangement, three types of samples were prepared, Fe-MOF $\{\mathrm{\bar{F}e^{I I I}}_{2}\mathrm{Fe^{II}}(\mu_{3}{\bf-O})(\mathrm{\bar{C}H}_{3}{\bf C O}_{2})_{6}(\mathrm{\bar{H}}_{2}{\bf O})_{3}$ as SBUs and $[1,1^{\prime}3^{\prime},1^{\prime\prime}$ -terphenyl]- $^{4,4^{\prime\prime},5^{\prime}}$ -tricarboxylic acid $\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{L}\right)$ as ligand}, Im@Fe-MOF for physically adsorbed Im molecules in the pore, and Im-Fe-MOF for coordinated Im molecules in the metal center. Pristine Fe-MOF, with three coordinated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules in the metal cluster, has high structural stability in the wide $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ range of 1 to 12. It shows two types of cage: six metal clusters for cage 1 (accessible diameter $\mathrm{i}\hat{3}.2\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ and 12 metal clusters for cage 2 (accessible diameter 22.1 Å) with $72.3\%$ void space. The Im molecules can neutrally replace the coordinated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in Fe-MOF for Im-Fe-MOF, which means the existence of a hydrogen atom in the nitrogen atom. However, the accessible diameter and free volume of ImFe-MOF has decreased up to $5.6\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ and $66.7\%$ , respectively. For $\operatorname{Im}(\omega\mathrm{Fe-MOF};$ , a simple soaking method was performed, and whether the Im molecules are coordinated was confirmed by
XPS. The BET surface areas of three samples from ${\bf N}_{2}$ sorption at 77 K are 2109.62 for Fe-MOF, 1894.41 for Im@Fe-MOF, and $1627.71\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for Im-Fe-MOF, respectively. The conductivity was evaluated by AC impedance measurement at VT and humidity. At RT and high humidity $(98\%\ \mathrm{RH})$ , the three compounds show conductivity values of $2.56\times10^{-5}$ (Fe-MOF), $\phantom{+}8.41\phantom{+}\times10^{-5}$ $\left(\operatorname{Im}(\omega\mathrm{Fe-MOF}\right)$ , and $2.06\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ (Im-FeMOF). Furthermore, the conductivity of samples containing Im such as $\operatorname{Im}(\omega\mathrm{Fe-MOF}$ and Im-Fe-MOF improves up to $4.23\times$ $10^{-3}$ and $1.{\dot{2}}1\times10^{-2}{\mathrm{Scm}}^{-1}$ at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ respectively, However, the conductivity of $\operatorname{Im}(\omega\mathrm{Fe-MOF}$ significantly decreased after one washing with water despite Im-Fe-MOF not changing at all. The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ determined by VT conductivity was $0.385~\mathrm{eV}$ for Fe-MOF, $0.573\ \mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathrm{Im}(\varpi\mathrm{Fe-MOF}.$ , and $0.436~\mathrm{eV}$ for Im-Fe-MOF. To understand the origin of conductivity, a density functional theory (DFT) calculation was performed using a simple model of Fe-MOF and Im-Fe-MOF. The replacement of coordinated $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ with Im is an exothermic reaction $(-23\mathrm{\kcal\mol^{-1}})$ in aqueous solution. The coordinated Im can contribute more to the proton conductivity due to the low proton dissociation energy $\mathrm{(N{-}H,~{-}96.8~\dot{\ k c a l}~m o l^{-1}},$ ) than that of water $(\mathrm{O-H},$ $-121.1\mathrm{\kcal\mol^{-1}},$ ). In other words, the calculation results propose that the coordinated Im provides structural stability but also donates a proton easily. Therefore, the coordinated Im and guest $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ have a dominant Grotthuss mechanism with partial molecular diffusion for proton conduction. A similar study with opposite results was performed using (NENU-3, $(\bar{\big[}\bar{\mathrm{C}}\mathrm{u}_{12}\big(\mathrm{BTC}\big)_{8}\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\big)_{12}\big]\big[\mathrm{HPW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}\big]\big)$ ·guest) by Ye et al. in 2017.146 NENU-3 is isostructural with HKUST-1 incorporating the POM anion. The axial sites of the Cu paddle wheel structure in NENU-3 initially occupied by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ are replaced by Im by coordinative modification $\mathrm{'Im-Cu}@\mathrm{NENU}-3\mathrm{a})$ . The sample containing free Im $(\mathrm{Im}@\mathrm{NENU})$ still has a coordinated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in the metal center and is only filled inside the pore (Figure 25). The loading amount is 14.5 wt $\%$ , which is approximately 36.75 Im molecules per unit cell. The conductivity was measured with pelletized samples under controlled humidity and temperature. At $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $90\%$ RH, the conductivity of Im−Cu@NENU-3a is $3.16\times10^{-4}\mathrm{{S}\ c m^{-1}}$ , whereas that of $\mathrm{Im}@\mathrm{NENU}-3$ is 2 orders of magnitude higher $\mathrm{'1.82\times10^{-2}S~c m^{-1}})$ than that of the free Im sample. The value is higher than previous results of Fe-based
MOFs $\bf\langle I m\mathcal{Q}F e{\mathrm{-}}M O F$ and Im-Fe-MOF). To obtain an intuitive insight for proton conductivity, the single-crystal structure of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Im-Cu}\ @\left(\mathrm{NENU}-3a\right)$ was analyzed after exposure to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ vapor. The result shows that an isolated lattice $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ is located in the octahedral cage with a distance of 3.819 Å from the $\mathbf{N}$ atom of Im. Moreover, lattice $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules are independent in the channel. Due to the immobile Im through the regular coordination, the reduced space isolates the lattice water and the proton conduction by self-diffusion is interrupted resulting in the low conductivity in $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Im-Cu}\ @\left(\mathrm{NENU}-3a\right)$ . By contrast, the $\mathrm{Im}@\mathrm{NENU}-3$ achieves successive H-bond networks among the free Im molecules and guest $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules, thus facilitating the proton transport. Compared to the previous result of Fe-based MOFs, this study demonstrates that the inclusion of free Im molecules can render high conductivity, which is attributed to the difference in structure and the spacial reason in the pore. Im-Fe-MOF has a 1D channel with enough distance between the terminal Im molecules and obtains a continuous H-bond network with adsorbed $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . However, Im$\mathrm{Cu}(\varpi(\mathrm{NENU}{-}3\mathrm{a})$ has a limited interconnection and encapsulation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ guests by a cage-type structure. Although the functionalization of metal centers and organic linkers is obviously an efficient strategy for promoting proton conductivity, more examples with different structures and functional groups are required for future endeavors.
## 4.3. Acid Guest Molecule Inclusion
For high proton conductivity in MOFs, the simplest way to increase carrier concentration is the synthesis of the inherent acidic framework or encapsulation of the acidic guest molecules (HCl, ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4},$ ${\mathrm{H}}_{3}{\mathrm{PO}}_{4},$ $\mathrm{CsHSO}_{4},$ , $\mathrm{CF}_{3}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ , and $p$ - $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H})$ into the pore space. Nonetheless, the application of impregnation of acidic molecules in most coordination compounds is challenging because the acidic species are detrimental to the coordination frameworks. In general, coordination bonds are dissociative under an acidic environment, resulting in structure decomposition. Therefore, extraordinary structural stability is a prerequisite for nonvolatile acid guest impregnation. The acidic solution inclusion has been widely studied in organic polymers with dangling acid functional groups in the backbone such as Nafion or poly(benzimidazole).147,148 Furthermore, there is a previous report for anisotropic proton conductivity in organic molecular porous materials, cucurbit[6]uril (CB[6]) with HCl inclusion. 49 In addition, MOFs constructed with an acidic backbone like phosphonate frameworks have been intensively studied for proton conductivity.43 In 2012, Ponomareva et al. tried to impregnate an acid moiety $\mathrm{(H}_{2}S O_{4}$ and ${\mathrm{H}}_{3}{\mathrm{PO}}_{4,}$ ) in highly porous and stable MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}})$ for high proton conductivity at intermediate temperatures (Figure 26).30 To avoid deterioration of the proton conductivity originating from the extended microstructure of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ above the BP, nonvolatile strong acids ( $\mathrm{\cdotH}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ and ${\mathrm{H}}_{3}{\mathrm{PO}}_{4,}$ ) with a high bp are the best candidates. The acid ${\mathcal{O}}\mathrm{MIL}{-}101\$ hybrids were prepared with an aqueous solution of ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ $(2.4\ \mathrm{M})$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}$ $(2.6\mathrm{~M})$ , r e s u l t i n g i n $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\right)_{4.47}\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)_{10}@\mathrm{MIL}-101$ and $\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4}\right)_{3.69}^{-}\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)_{5}@\mathrm{ML}.101$ formula units. The inclusion process is reversible and complete removal of acid is possible by washing with water. In the impedance spectra at high temperature, two semicircles are observed in the high-frequency region, which implies a bulk and GB resistance. At low temperature and $20\%$ RH, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}101$ has a quite high conductivity of $4.0\times10^{-2}$ and $2.5\times10^{-4}\mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ for ${\mathrm{H}}_{3}{\mathrm{PO}}_{4}@$ MIL-101. However, the temperature increase does not seem to be significantly effective for conductivity in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}101$ . In contrast, $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}101$ shows a gradual increase in conductivity with a convex profile, which can be explained by the loss of lattice $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules. $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}-101$ and ${\mathrm{H}}_{3}{\mathrm{PO}}_{4}@$ MIL-101 still indicate high proton conductivity of $1\times10^{-2}$ and $3\times10^{-3}\mathrm{Scm^{-1}}$ at $150{}^{\circ}\mathrm{\bar{C}},$ respectively. The conductivity in the cooling process was linear without significant change. The second heating−cooling cycle presents a similar trend with $E_{\mathrm{a}}=$ $0.42\ \mathrm{eV}$ .
The distinct difference in conductivity between the heating and cooling process is attributed to the kinetics of the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorption or desorption. An important point in the use of alternative acid-conducting media is that the hydration and dehydration processes do not affect the acid content. Nevertheless, the high humidity in $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}101$ is substantially contributing to the conducting pathway and facilitating the deprotonation of phosphoric acid. As a result, $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}101$ has a conductivity of $\mathrm{{i}.0\times10^{-2}\ S\ c m^{-1}}$ at $140~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ( $1.1\%$ RH) with $E_{\mathrm{a}}=0.25\ \mathrm{eV}$ . The remarkable stability of the H-bond between $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ -acid above $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is thought to be a strong capillary effect.
In 2014, Dybtsev and coworkers added triflic acid $\left(\mathrm{CF}_{3}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H},\right.$ TfOH) and toluenesulfonic acid $(p{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{\cdot}$ , TsOH) to MIL-101 $\mathrm{\langle}\mathrm{[}\mathrm{Cr}_{3}(\mathrm{O})(\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{BDC})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]$ (BDC $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ benzene1,4-dicarboxylate).150 For impregnation of the acids, the crystalline MIL-101 was immersed in $4.12\mathrm{M}$ aqueous solutions of TfOH and $2.2\mathrm{~M~}$ toluenesulfonic acids, which is the same method as a previous report on $\mathrm{H}_{2}S\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4}$ . From ICP
AES and elemental analysis (EA), 6.7 of TfOH molecules and 15 $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ were verified per MIL-101 formula units, while 1.1 of TsOH was included in MIL-101. The low $\mathrm{TsOH}$ contents are thought to be low stability of $\mathrm{TsOH}@\mathrm{MIL}\cdot101$ . The conductivity was recorded at $25\mathrm{-}65~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under $15\%$ , $50\%$ , and $100\%$ RH. TfOH@MIL-101 has the highest conductivity of $8\times$ $10^{-2}\mathrm{{S}\ c m^{-1}}$ and $\bar{5}\times10^{-2}\mathrm{{Scm}^{-1}}$ at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under $100\%$ and $15\%$ RH, respectively, where the discrepancy in conductivity by hydration is negligible. In addition, the $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ for proton diffusion in TfOH@MIL-101 is $0.25~\mathrm{eV}$ at $50\%$ RH and 0.23 at $15\%$ RH, indicating a typical Grotthuss mechanism. However, the lower acid content and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules of $\mathrm{TsOH}@\mathrm{MIL}.101$ are reflected in the poor conductivity value of $3\times10^{-5}\mathrm{{Scm}^{-1}}$ at 50 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under $100\%$ RH. In contrast to $\mathrm{TfOH}@\mathrm{MIL}\cdot101$ , the conductivity and $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ of $\mathrm{TsOH}@\mathrm{MIL}\ –101$ are strongly affected by hydration. The proton migration mechanism in ${\mathrm{TfOH}}\ @\mathbf{MIL}-$ 101 was investigated by $\mathrm{^{1}\bar{H}\ N M R}$ at low temperature ranges from 180 to $290~\mathrm{K}.$ The intense and narrow symmetric signal belongs to a water and acidic proton with high mobility, and an asymmetric wide signal can be assigned by an immobile proton in the ligand. The calculated number of mobile protons by integration intensity of NMR signal is $48\pm5$ per formula unit, which is in good agreement with the chemical composition of TfOH@MIL-101. The width of the narrow line became larger as the temperature decreased. The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ from line width analysis is coincident with one from impedance measurement, indicating the proton transfer between the neighbor guest molecules $\left(\mathrm{H}^{2}\mathrm{O},\right.$ $\mathrm{H}^{3}\dot{\mathrm{O}}^{+},$ , and $\mathrm{TfO^{-}}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ). A further study of the H-bond structure was carried out in VT-IR spectroscopy. The broad peak maximum of TfOH@MIL-101 $(33\bar{1}0\mathrm{cm}^{-1}),$ ) was red-shifted from liquid $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ which can be observed in supercooled $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ .151 Below $200\mathrm{K},$ the fine structures of the $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ cluster appear with complicated peaks. One of the important points is that TfOH@MIL-101 is missing the signal at $3700~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ corresponding to free $_\mathrm{O-H}$ stretching, which implies that the all protons participate in the H-bond.
The synergistic superprotonic conductivity was studied in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}.101.\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ by Li et al. in 2017.152 This research combined the acid functional groups of the framework and the nonvolatile acid molecule inclusion strategy. Depending on the location of the Brønsted acid source, three types of samples were prepared for comparison: MIL- $101.5\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ (framework), $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}-101$ (guest), and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}.101.\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ (framework $^+$ guest). In the proton conductivity measurement, the conductivity was proportional to humidity due to increased hydrophilicity by the sulfonic acid group. At $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $90\%$ RH, the conductivity of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}.101.\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ $(3\mathrm{~M})$ $3.55\times$ $10^{-1}{\mathrm{S}}{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}.$ ) was 4 orders of magnitude higher than that of MIL$101..\mathrm{{SO}}_{3}\mathrm{{H}}$ at the same condition. The superprotonic conductivity is mainly thought to be the high density of Brønsted acidic site $\left(-S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H}\right)$ . As the temperature rises to $90\ {}^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the maximum conductivity values of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}.101.5\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ (3 M), $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}\cdot101$ $(3\mathrm{~M~})$ , and $\mathrm{MIL-}101{\cdot}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ are 1.82, $6.09\times10^{-1}.$ , and $6.32\times10^{-5}\mathrm{{S}\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}}$ , respectively. Furthermore, the conductivity was evaluated at subzero temperatures ( $-10$ to $-40^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ ) due to the importance of the subfreezing operation of fuel cell systems. $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}@\mathrm{MIL}.101{-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ $(3~\mathrm{M})$ maintains a high value of $0.92\times10^{-2}\mathrm{{S}\ c m^{-1}}$ at $-40{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with a low $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ (0.21 $\mathrm{eV}$ ). A small difference in conductivity can be derived from the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules freezing. The origine of the excellent conducting properties in the wide temperature range should be the multiple proton sources.
Phang and coworkers investigated the pH-dependent protonconducting behavior in MOF-74 (Ni), $\left[\mathrm{Ni}_{2^{-}}(\hat{\mathrm{dobdc}})(\hat{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\right]$ · $\mathsf{6H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\begin{array}{l}{{\bigl(\mathrm{dobdc}^{4-}\ =\ 2,5}}\end{array}$ -dioxido-1,4-benzenedicarboxylate; $\mathrm{C}_{8}\mathrm{O}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{2})$ .31 MOF-74 compounds have one coordinated solvent in the metal center. At different $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ values (5.7−1.8) of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S\mathrm{O}_{4}$ aqueous solutions, soaking $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobdc}\big)$ results in the $\mathrm{H}^{+}@$ $\bar{\bf N i}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ , where the structure is stable to $\mathrm{\ttpH1.8.}$ . In fact, the ${\mathrm{SO}_{4}}^{2-}$ anion might exist in the framework for charge balance, but it is impossible to detect with EDX and EA. Meanwhile, $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ that leached from the framework was detected, which means part of the ligands was present in an anionic form in the pore. The proton conductivity of pristine MOF-74 $\mathrm{(Ni)}$ is $5.3\times10^{-5}\mathrm{~S~}$ $\mathsf{\bar{c}m}^{-1}$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $95\%$ RH and $1.4\times10^{-4}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . These conductivities originated from proton donation of coordinated acidic $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ at the metal center with guest $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . As the $\mathsf{p H}$ decreases, the conductivities were $5.3\times\mathrm{i}0^{-4}$ for pH 5.7, $5.1\bar{\times}10^{-3}$ for $\mathsf{p H4.l}$ , $1.3\times10^{-2}$ for pH 3.3, $1.9\times10^{-2}$ for $\mathrm{pH}2.4$ , and $2.2\times\mathrm{\bar{~}}10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{pH}1.8.$ To understand the environment of protonated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , the $_\mathrm{O-H}$ stretching vibration mode in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was studied with IR spectroscopy. The characteristic peaks of $_\mathrm{O-H}$ at 3660 and $\hat{3}572~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are redshifted as acidified due to the weakened $_\mathrm{O-H}$ bond by H-bond interaction.153 The $_\mathrm{O-H}$ peaks disappear by the activation process at $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and all material properties are recovered to the original state. Therefore, it is evident that the acidified $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ cluster in the robust framework plays a key role for high proton conductivity.
In 2017, Yang and coworkers reported high proton conductivity in a $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})$ -based flexible MOF, $\left({\mathrm{BUT}}({\mathrm{Cr}})\right)$ , $\mathrm{Cr}_{3}(\mu_{3}{\mathrm{-O}})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}(\mathrm{NDC}(\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{5/6})_{2})_{3};$ $\mathrm{\DeltaNDC}(S O_{3}H)_{2}\ =\ 4,8-$ disulfonaphthalene-2,6-dicarboxylatlate) with a high density of sulfonic acid.33 Previously, proton-conductive MOFs were focused on the robust framework with a high proton conductivity of about $10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . As a result, the rigid frameworks cannot respond to humidity change by breaking the conducting pathway under the low humidity condition. However, a flexible framework might respond to external $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ pressure stimuli without loss of H-bond. The BUT-8 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ is a 3D structure with a 1D channel along the $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{c}}$ -axis (Figure 27). The sulfonic acid functional groups surround the pore surface with the incorporation of $\mathrm{\bar{NH}}_{2}(\mathrm{\bar{C}H}_{3})_{2}{}^{+}$ . The countercations are replaced with $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ by immersing in a 0.5 M ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{3}$ aqueous solution for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ (BUT-8 $(\mathrm{Cr})\mathrm{A})$ , which was confirmed by IR measurement. The BUT-8 $(\mathrm{Cr})\mathrm{A}$ is intact in a wide $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ range even at the high concentration $(\sim10\mathrm{M})$ of acidic solution. The PXRD patterns of BUT-8 $(\mathrm{Cr})\mathrm{A}$ were shifted to a low angle with color change under the humidified condition ( $\angle65\%$ RH), which is reversible with hydration and dehydration. No more structural expansion was observed above $65\%$ RH. In the comparison of the conductivity with a rigid framework $(\mathrm{ML}.101\:(\mathrm{C\bar{r}}).5\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H})$ , the conductivity values of BUT-8 $(\mathrm{Cr})\mathrm{A}$ and BUT-8 $\displaystyle\left(\mathbf{Cr}\right)$ were $6.3~\times~10^{-3}~\dot{\mathrm{~S~}}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1.3~\times~10^{-3}$ $\boldsymbol{s}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at $65\%$ RH, respectively. In contrast, the rigid (MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right){\cdot}S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H})$ framework indicated $5.92\times10^{-5}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ . Furthermore, they exhibit two orders of difference $(10^{-7}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ for flexible and $10^{-9}\mathrm{{S}\ c m^{-1}}$ for rigid framework) at $11\%$ RH. At high humidity ( $100\%$ RH) and ${\bf\80}~^{\circ}\bf C,$ the conductivities o $\mathrm{\Delta\f\MIL{-}101{-}S O_{3}H,}$ ${\bf{B U T}}{\cdot}8{\left({\bf{C r}}\right)},$ , and BUT- $8(\mathrm{Cr})\mathrm{A}$ increased to $1.6\times10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ $\mathrm{'}E_{\mathrm{a}}=0.23\:\mathrm{eV})$ , $4.63\times10^{-2}\mathrm{{S}\ c m^{-1}}$ $\mathrm{'}E_{\mathrm{a}}=0.21\ \mathrm{eV})$ , and $1.27\times$ $10^{-1}~\mathrm{{S}~c m^{-1}}$ $'{E_{\mathrm{a}}}=0.11~\mathrm{eV})$ , respectively. The $\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ -mediated conductivities are $2.4\times10^{-2}\mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ for BUT- $8(\mathrm{Cr})\mathrm{A}$ and $5.0\times$ $10^{-3}\mathrm{{Scm}^{-1}}$ for BUT- $8(\mathrm{Cr})$ (with $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ values of 0.25 and $0.31\mathrm{eV}_{;}$ , respectively) due to the isotope effect with low mobility. These results demonstrate the good correlation between structural flexibility and proton conductivity. In addition, the high carrier density through cation replacement and its outstanding structural stability are beneficial for excellent proton conductivity.
## 4.4. External Stimuli to Control the Proton Conductivity: Pressure and Light
External stimuli such as pressure and light can induce a new physical property of materials not seen under normal environmental conditions. In particular, the design of tunable ionconductive solids is important and of interest due to their various applications in energy conversion devices and biological mimic systems.154 Thus, the proton-conductive solids, which respond to external stimuli, provide the opportunity to communicate the biology system and to develop novel materials. To date, however, most of studies on proton-conductive MOFs have been focused on the structural factors and increased carrier concentration (acidity) to achieve high proton conductivity through humidity control. Therefore, examples of other external stimuli for proton-conductive MOFs are a handful. In this section, we focus on the study of proton conductivity controlled by external stimuli such as mechanical pressure and light.
The pressure induced amorphization of $\mathrm{Zn(HPO_{4})}$ - $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}\right)_{2}\cdot\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Im}\right)_{2}^{91}$ and its effect on proton conductivity were investigated by Umeyama and coworkers in $2014.^{155}$ In general, it is noteworthy that the preparation of sample contact for the measurement of the pressure-induced proton conductivity is notoriously challenging. Previously, the pressuredependent conductivity has been studied in various SSPCs such as clay,156 oxides,157 and cesium oxyacid $\mathrm{(CsHSO_{4})}$ ,158 but pressure-induced proton conductivity in MOFs has not been extensively studied. According to previous studies, when the pressure applied on the compound was a few ${\mathrm{GPa}}_{,}$ , the conductivity tended to be low and the altered $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ and the conduction mechanism are changed. In this study, the effect of pressure on proton conductivity and the Ea values of $\mathrm{\bar{Zn}(H P O_{4})(H_{2}P O_{4})_{2}.2H_{2}i m}$ are characterized with in situ XRD analysis. The pristine compound has an H-bond network between phosphonate and imidazolium with a conductivity of $1.3\times10^{-4}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}=0.47~\mathrm{eV}\right)$ at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and ambient pressure. Considering the nonporous crystal structure, the intrinsic conductivity presents the Grotthuss mechanism without guest molecules. The pressure-dependent conductivity is measured with a cubic-anvil high-pressure apparatus (DIA) at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and the pressure is calibrated by measuring the electronic conductivity of bismuth(Bi) metal (Figure 28). The conductivity starts to decrease as the pressure increase up to $3\mathrm{GPa}$ and then remains constant up to 6 GPa (Figure 28). In the pressure range from 6 to $7\mathrm{GPa}$ , the conductivity shows a small increase. The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ at 7 GPa was $0.84~\mathrm{{\eV}}.$ . The decreased conductivity and increased $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ are strongly correlated to the altered conducting pathway by structural change. From in situ XRD pattern, a structural transition with broad peaks and an increased amorphous phase is observed even below $0.1\mathrm{GPa}$ . At $4.1\mathrm{GPa},$ , the Bragg peaks disappear with complete amorphization and no more changes are observed up to $10\mathrm{GPa}$ (pressureinduced amorphization). There is still an argument in better conductivity of the amorphous and crystalline phase with contradicting results. The decrease conductivity of $\bar{Z}\mathrm{n(HPO_{4})}$ - $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}\right)_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{\bar{H}}_{2}\mathrm{im}$ is strongly correlated to amorphization, leading to the dense structure, which might obstruct the uniform proton chemical potential and molecular motion by spatial reduction because the rotational motion of imidazolium in the compound is a key factor for proton diffusion. The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ values became larger than that of the ambient condition and proposes less potential proton conduction by creating trapping sites or restricted molecular motion.
Indeed, the optical stimuli can be one of the factors for tuning the proton conductivity and applied in the various energy conversion. For example, the photoinduced proton gradient in a silica matrix sol−gel is applied in artificial ATP synthesis.159 Most recently, Nagarkar et al. have investigated the photoacid (pyranine) incorporation in the glassy MOF, $\mathrm{Z\bar{n}(H P O_{4})}$ - $(\mathrm{\tilde{H}}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{2}{\cdot}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Im}\hat{)}_{2},$ to control proton conductivity by light irradiation (Figure 29).160 Photoacid molecules are molecules containing protons that can be reversibly released on light irradiation .161 The doping of photoacid in glassy MOF can be used to control $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ through the light. After absorbing the light $\left(<450\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ , the $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ of the photoacid, trisodium 8-hydroxy1,3,6-pyrenetrisulfonate (pyranine) changes from 7.4 to 1.3. For highly efficient light absorption in photoacid, the host material should be transparent. Therefore, a glassy MOF is beneficial as a host material and $5\mathrm{mol}\%$ of pyranine was doped in the melted MOF. The pyranine does not show any crystalline peak after doping in MOF. X-ray absorption spectra (XAS) confirmed that the coordination environment of $\bar{Z}\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ does not change. The $Z\mathrm{n-O}$ distance obtained from radial distribution function (RDF) data is not affected by pyranine inclusion, which means an identical $Z\mathbf{n}$ -phosphate 1D framework. In addition, protonated imidazole molecules show the same $\mathrm{\DeltaN-H}$ signal with pristine MOF in the IR spectrum. Consequently, the doping process does not change overall structural components.
In 2018, Müller and coworkers designed azobenzene-based surface-mounted MOFs (SURMOFs) for switchable proton conductivity by light (Figure 30). The azobenzene group can undergo the photoinduced cis−trans isomerization by UV irradiation, reversibly. In a previous study, azobenzene-based MOFs have been studied in the remote controllable adsorption/ diffusion and gas mixture separation.162−164 Thus, there was no study on proton conductivity control of MOFs using the azobenzene moiety. However, the use of photochromic molecules for the conductivity switching has been widely studied in an organic semiconductor,165 carbon nanotubes,166 and graphene.167 In this research, the SURMOF of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\big(\bar{\mathrm{F}_{2}}\mathrm{AzoBDC}\big)_{2}\big(\mathrm{dabco}\big)$ impregnated 1,4-butanediol or 1,2,3-triazole molecules in the pore as conducting media because of the suitable size, functionality with polar character, and its proton conducting property.
The irradiation with ${530}\mathrm{nm}$ (green light) induces trans to cis photoisomerization $(86\%)$ in the azobenzene group (cis state). In contrast, $400~\mathrm{nm}$ (violet light) irradiation renders a reverse trans form of $87\%$ . The conductivity was measured by impedance spectra in the frequency range of $0.5{-}5\mathrm{MHz}$ . The SURMOF thickness of $150\mathrm{nm}$ showed a conductivity of $10^{-8}S$ $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ without any conducting media in Ar atmosphere, where the photoisomerization is not effective. The 1,4-butanediol or 1,2,3-triazole molecules are loaded in the gas flow, and the change in the PXRD peak area ratio reflects the conducting media incorporation in the SURMOF. The loading amounts of guest molecules estimated from quartz crystal microbalance (QCM) are $1/3$ molecule (butanediol) per pore and 1 molecule (triazole) per pore. In butanol loading, initially the current increases ${\sim}2$ nA at a voltage of $1\mathrm{V}$ and an applied frequency of 1 $\mathbf{Hz},$ but $35\%$ of the current decreases after light irradiation with ${\mathfrak{s}}30\ \mathrm{nm}$ (cis form). Sequentially, irradiation with $430\mathrm{nm}$ (trans form) recovers the initial current. In addition, there is no significant loss of conductivity in repeated cycling. The photoswitchable proton conductivities in a butanediol medium are $9.0\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~S~}_{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ for trans and $6.1\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ for cis. Further measurements with a change in conducting medium to 1,2,3-triazole molecules were performed as an extension. The modulated conductivities of the 1,2,3-triazole $@$ $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\big(\mathrm{F}_{2}\mathrm{AzoBDC}\big)_{2}\big(\mathrm{dabc}$ o) were $1.2\times10^{-4}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ (trans) and $7.9\times10^{-5}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ (cis). Compared with pristine SURMOF, guest loading clearly contributed to the conductivity enhancement and photoisomers provide a different environment with a discrepancy in conductivity. To understand the mechanism, the plausible interaction between the guest molecules and photoisomers was investigated through quantum chemical calculations with a model of a single $\bar{\mathrm{Cu}_{2}}(\bar{\mathrm{F}_{2}}\mathrm{AzoBDC})_{2}$ (dabco) pore including two azo side groups in either trans or cis mode and loading of a butanediol or a triazole molecule. In the computational calculation, all guests have a good H-bond interaction with the $\mathbf{N}$ atom in the azo group and a strong Hbond is observed in the cis isomer than trans with $35\mathrm{meV}$ of BE difference. The strong binding in cis might cause reduced molecular mobility resulting in lower conductivity with proton trapping. Furthermore, the increase in the H-bond strength in cis was experimentally verified with decreases in the vibration wavenumbers of the $-\mathrm{OH}$ $({\sim}40~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ and the $-\mathrm{NH}$ $_{\sim10}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ ) bands by the IR spectra.
In this section, the dynamic control of proton conductivity was discussed with a few examples. In fact, the absolute conductivity value and the degree of conductivity variation are not significant. However, there is a room for further progress in the study of proton conductivity related to external stimuli.
## 4.5. Defect Control for Proton Conduction
Intrinsic and extrinsic defects in a solid material play an unambiguous role in physical properties such as magnetic, electronic, and optical properties. Therefore, defect control can be a good way of adjusting physical properties. In solid crystalline MOFs, intrinsic and extrinsic defects can be understood in terms of the entropy aspect with the equilibrium state. Some of the postsynthetic modifications in metals and ligands replacement can be examples of defect creation.169,170 In addition, in the synthetic procedure additives induce a partial disconnection between metal centers without an overall structural change.171 The addition of defects in MOFs was studied for fine-tuning of sorption or catalytic properties. In particular, when the number of vacant positions exceeds the number of available ions. ion mobility is high with a relatively low $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ for migration between these positions. Consequently, a comprehensive understanding of defect control is of significant importance in the fine-tuning of a structure for the desired application.
In 2015, Taylor et al. made a systematic defect control in UiO66 for proton conduction.58 UiO-66 consists of hexazirconium(IV) oxo hydroxyl cluster connected by 12-terephthalic acid. Thus, the metal/ligand ratio or fatty acid addition as an additive might generate a nonbridging ligand defect in the metal cluster, encouraging proton conductivity. For sample preparation, two types of approaches were applied; (I) metal-to-ligand ratio control (1) 6:6 (2) 6:4 (3) 6:3, and (II) monocarboxylic additive use (4) acetic acid, (5) low, and (6) high concentration of stearic acid. The carboxylate defect amounts determined by the EA and TGA measurements were 0.6 (1), 1.6 (2), 2.8 (3), 1.4 (4), 0.4 (5) and 1 (6) per $\mathrm{Zr}$ cluster. The different levels of defects in six samples changed their porosity. Samples $_{1-3}$ showed similar porosity with $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ uptake amounts of $12.2\mathrm{-}13.3\$ mmol $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{g}^{-1},}\end{array}$ , whereas the fatty acid additive samples 4−6 had a considerably enhanced porosity with ${\bf N}_{2}$ uptake of $14.4\ \mathrm{mmol}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ for (4), $\mathrm{16.0\mmol{g}^{-1}}$ for (5), and $17.9\mathrm{\mmol\g^{-1}}$ for (6) at $P/P_{0}=0.97.$ In particular, the high-porosity samples indicate the relatively high $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ uptake at $298\ \mathrm{K}.$ In impedance analysis, all samples indicate two semicircles of bulk and GB. As defects increase from (1) to (3), the conductivity proportionally increased from $1.30\times10^{-5}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ for (1) to $\bar{1}.0\bar{1}\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~\bar{~}}$ $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ for (3) at $65~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ varying from 0.25 to $0.36~\mathrm{eV}.$ . In sample (4), acetate covers the Lewis acid sites and has a relatively low conductivity of $2.75\times10^{-5}\mathrm{Scm^{-1}}$ $\mathrm{'}E_{\mathrm{a}}=0.29\:\mathrm{eV})$ . In contrast, the stearic acid addition improved the conductivity to $2.63\times10^{-4}$ and $6.93\times10^{-3}\mathrm{{Scm}^{-1}}$ , with $E_{\mathrm{a}}=0.32$ and 0.22 $\mathrm{^\bulletv}$ for (5) and for (6), respectively. The highest conductivity among the six samples was achieved in excess of stearic acid addition during the synthesis. Comparing samples with similar defect levels and different porosity (1 and 5, and 2 and 6), it is unequivocal that the high conductivity is significantly correlated to the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ uptake by porosity. In addition, the increase in $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ from $0.25\ \mathrm{eV}$ for (1) to $0.36~\mathrm{eV}$ for (3) can be explained by a Coulombic interaction between the charge carrier and the pore surface due to the increased proton concentration within similar porosity. The proton mobility was evaluated by pulsed-field gradient solid-state $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR (PFG-SSNMR) measurements using a stimulated echo (STE) experiment. The diffusion constant of a mobile proton for (6) is $4.0\times10^{-7}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}.$ . However, sample (3) was in the out-of-detection range. From these results, it observed that defect control improves the conductivity up to 3 orders of magnitude. A vacant site by ligand defect can be occupied with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ and increased pore volume simultaneously promotes proton mobility. In the same year, Taylor et al. reported the role of an ordered defect sublattice on the acidity of sulfonated MOF (Figure 31).59 For this study, an acid-functionalized MOF (zirconium 2-sulfoterephthalate) was used for the introduction of ligand defects because the charge carrier density and mobility are key factors for high proton conductivity, although the ligand defect loses some carrier concentration. The structure analysis was performed by powder Rietveld refinement, where the space group and cell parameters were cubic $\overline{{I m\overline{{3}}}}$ and $\mathrm{~a~}=\ 41.\bar{490}\bar{(6)}$ , respectively. This cell parameter is approximately two times larger than that of the related zirconium terephthalate (UiO-66) cell constant $\overset{\cdot}{\underset{\cdot}{a}}=\overset{\cdot}{\underset{\cdot}{\longrightarrow}}$
$20.7004(2)\mathring{\mathrm{A}},F m\overline{{3}}m)$ because the vacant metal cluster sites are located in six of the eight corners of the UiO-66 cell (Figure 31). Therefore, two types of SBUs (12-connected clusters and 9- connected clusters) are present, resulting in three kinds of pore $(0.5~\mathrm{\nm}$ diameter tetrahedral pores, ${\sim}0.6~\mathrm{\nm}$ diameter octahedral pores, and $1.2\times2.5~\mathrm{nm}$ large pores from the vacant site). However, the pore of the final structure corresponding to ICP analysis $\left(\mathrm{Zr}/\mathrm{S}~=~1.43\right)$ is not well-defined due to the reduced atomic occupancy in the ligand. Their porosity was estimated by $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ sorption at $77\mathrm{K}$ with the activation process of solvent exchange and RT vacuum because the sample undergoes a significant loss in porosity depending on the sample activation temperatures. Consequently, three well-defined pores (0.59, 0.73, and $1.71~\mathrm{nm}\dot{}$ ) were observed from ${\bf N}_{2}$ sorption. The VT impedance measurements $\left(15\mathrm{-}65^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\right)$ were carried out on the two batches of the sample under $95\%$ RH. At $65\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ both samples show analogous conductivity values, $1.93\times10^{-3}$ and $1.8\dot{2}\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ with $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ of $0.25~\mathrm{eV}$ . Despite the sufficiently high conductivity value, this value is approximately one order lower than other MOFs under the same measurement condition. It seems that reduced conductivity originated from the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ concentration loss resulting from the ligand missing. Nevertheless, the decrease in conductivity with low humidity is insignificant $\mathrm{7.15\times10^{-4}}$ and $2.75\times\mathrm{~10^{-4}~S~c m^{-1}}$ at $95\%$ RH and $30\%$ RH, respectively, at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\dot{}$ ; the value at low humidity condition indicate a hydrophilic nature of zirconium 2- sulfoterephthalate with high conductivity. To understand the proton-trapping phenomenon resulting in low conductivity at high humidity, a DFT calculation was performed with various model structures with defects (d) and defect-free (df). The results show that ${\mathrm{d}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{SO}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}$ had the lowest $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ value $\left(-2.3\right)$ comparable to strong acid. In contrast, df- $S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ has a weaker acidity $\left(\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}=-0.6\right)$ . Furthermore, $\mathrm{\Delta\d{-}Z r O H}$ can act as a strong proton-trapping site with the highest $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ of 13.3, while protontrapping in $\mathrm{df-ZrOH}$ results in $-7.7\mathrm{~pK}_{\mathrm{a}}$ which is invalid. Therefore, the calculation suggested that the conductivity could be enhanced by the trapping of protons in the defective site. For further study, three types of acid (acetic acid, sulfoacetic acid, and $0.1\mathrm{M}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{O}_{4},$ ) were added to the defective sample. At $65^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $95\%$ RH, the conductivities were $2.4\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ for acetic acid, $5.26\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ for sulfoacetic acid, and $3.46\times$ $10^{-3}\mathrm{{Scm}^{-1}}$ for 0.1 M ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}S{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ with a similar $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ of about $0.25\mathrm{eV}.$ , respectively. The sulfoacetic acid sample had a high conductivity of $\bar{5}.26\times10^{-3}\mathrm{{Scm}^{-1}}$ under $40\%$ RH, but it decreased to $3.78\times$ $10^{-4}\ \mathrm{S}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ as temperature increase due to the metastable hydration.
In 2016, Inukai and coworkers reported the anhydrous proton conductivity by encapsulating the proton carrier in the structural defect.60 As we previously mentioned, high carrier concentration and mobility are key factors for high proton conductivity. The hydrated Nafion shows a good performance at a mild temperature, but $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ proton carrier escapes from the host material above the BP, resulting in poor conductivity. Therefore, the encapsulation of highly mobile proton carriers is an important factor and is challenging for a durable conductivity. For this study, 2D $\left[\mathrm{Zn}{-}\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{P}\check{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\big)_{2}^{\smile}(\mathrm{T}\mathbf{z})_{2}\right]_{n}{}^{45}$ was used, where bidentate $\mathrm{Tz}$ is bridging the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ion, forming a 2D-layer and monodentate ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{PO}}_{4}^{-}$ coordinated to $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ is located between layers (Figure 32). To introduce inherent defects, the different stoichiometric ratios (4.0 (2), 4.4 (3), 4.8 (4), and $5.2\ \mathrm{mmol}$ (reacted)) of $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4}$ are used in monodentate $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{-}$ network. In particular, incorporation of uncoordinated $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4}$ which has a rotational and translational motion, is good for proton transport. The degree of defects was evaluated by ICP and EA comparing with the defect-free compound. Although all the compounds differed a little in PXRD patterns and the relative intensity and had a small additional peak, overall patterns were analogous with the pristine, without structural change up to 150 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The proton conductivities were measured under ${\bf N}_{2}$ atmosphere at $30{-}150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The conductivity increased with the decreasing $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ as the ratio of reacted ${\mathrm{H}}_{3}{\mathrm{PO}}_{4}$ increased. The highest conductivity was $4.6\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}=0.53~\mathrm{eV}\right)$ for (5) at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ This conductivity could be retained for more than $900\mathrm{min}$ under the same conditions. In the $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{O}_{2}$ fuel cell test, the OCV for (5) at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was $0.88{\mathrm{~V}}$ , exhibiting a small gas crossover and an unfavored side reaction. The maximum power density was $2.8\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}.$ , which is the first demonstration of a fuel cell with an MOF solid-state electrolyte. A further observation of a mobile proton carrier was performed with extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) and SSNMR for (2) and (5). Despite the presence of a defect in the monodentate ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{PO}}_{4}^{-}$ framework in 2, there was no significant change in the coordination environment of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ( $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{\zn-O}$ or $\mathrm{Zn-}$ N) for (2) and (5) because $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ might be coordinated with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ instead of the monodentate ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{PO}}_{4}^{-}$ . Direct evidence for uncoordinated $\mathrm{{H_{3}P O_{4}}}$ was observed through cross-polarization magic angle spin (CP-MAS) NMR and MAS NMR of $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ and $\ensuremath{{}^{31}\mathrm{P}}$ . CP-MAS NMR shows only framework signals precluding the liquid phase mobile $\ensuremath{{}^{31}\mathrm{\overline{{P}}}}$ species. Although crystallographically only one type of monodentate $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{-}$ exists, two signals ( $\cdot-3.2$ and $-5.7\mathrm{\ppm})$ from CP-MAS NMR were observed, which means that there are two types of monodentate $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{-}$ with a different environment due to defects. Additional large peaks $\mathrm{(0.0\ppm}$ and $-11.4\mathrm{ppm}$ ) were observed in the $\ensuremath{{}^{31}\mathrm{P}}$ MAS NMR spectra for 4 and 5, which correspond to the $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4}$ $\left(0.5\mathrm{\ppm}\right)$ and ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{PO}}_{4}^{-}$ $\left(-12\mathrm{\ppm}\right)$ .172 In addition, proton dynamics was studied by ultrafast MAS solid-state $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ NMR for 2 and 5. 2 indicates four peaks at 8.0, 8.7 (assigned to the remaining two protons of $\mathrm{HTz}$ ), 11.0, and $12.3\ \mathrm{ppm}$ (two protons of monodentate $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}$ −and one proton of $\mathrm{HTz}$ $(\Nu-$ H). For compound 5, two new peaks (8.9 and $10.5~\mathrm{ppm}$ ) are assigned to uncoordinated mobile $\mathrm{{H_{3}P O_{4}}}$ and ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{PO}}_{4}{}^{-}$ . This was supported by there being no signal in the $\mathrm{^{1}H-^{1}H}$ double quantum NMR (DQNMR), which indicates low mobile and static species. Finally, the smaller $^{1}\mathrm{H}$ spin−lattice relaxation time 1 $(T_{\mathrm{1H}})$ of 5 than that of compound 2 indicates that the new proton-conducting pathway is derived from $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4},\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{-}$ , and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ .
Recently, Zhang and Fei studied the inherent missing metallinker connection in a robust sulfonated MOF (TMOF-2, $\left[\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{bipy})_{2}(1,4\mathrm{-BDMS})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{0.5}\right]_{n})$ for proton conductivity.173 TMOF-2, a 2-fold interpenetrated structure, has a single $\dot{\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ metal node with a distorted coordination geometry, where $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ - bipyridine (bipy) makes a square and 1,4-benzene-dimethanesulfonate (1,4-BDMS) are attached in the axial site. It has a high porosity of $37.2\%$ and a robust framework in a wide range of pH. In particular, one of two adjacent sulfonates is the missing connection with ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ providing a free sulfonate group that is deprotonated and contributes to the enhanced conductivity in the interconnected pore. As compared with the fully coordinated compound TMOF-1, TMOF-2 exhibits steep $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ vapor adsorption and hysteresis in the sorption profile due to the discrepancy of hydrophilicity and a strong affinity for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . Their proton conductivity is in good agreement with their hydrophilicity. TMOF-1 has a conductivity of $1.62\times10^{-6}$ S $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $\bar{'}_{E_{\mathrm{a}}=0.\dot{3}4\mathrm{eV}})$ and TMOF-2 shows $1.23\times10^{-4}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\left(E_{:}$ $\mathrm{~\boldmath~\Gamma~}=\mathrm{~\bf0.37~\eV})$ at $90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $98\%$ RH. Therefore, the regular sulfonate group defects and guest $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ create efficient H-bond networks with synergistic proton transfer.
From the results discussed above, it is clear that the intrinsic and extrinsic defects inevitably affect the proton conductivity through the formation of proton-conducting pathways. Nevertheless, to date, the role of defects and the effect of the crystal surface have not been considered significantly for conductivity. Therefore, the importance of exact defect control should be regarded in the study of proton-conductive MOFs as a future endeavor.
## 4.6. Hydrophilicity for Proton Conductivity
Water-mediated SSPCs (e.g., Nafion) require for a highhumidity condition for efficient performance. By contrast, anhydrous proton conductors (protic organic solvent and acid) need a high temperature to generate mobile proton species with high conductivity. In general, proton-conductive MOFs also follow a similar tendency and do not have high conductivity at the ambient condition in the relatively low humidity and temperature. In other words, most of MOFs’ conductivities are proportional to humidity and temperature. Nevertheless, hydrophilicity is a key factor for the affinity of water and can induce an efficient proton-conducting property even at relatively low humidity. Therefore, the control of hydrophilicity on the pore surface is of considerable importance for designing practical proton-conductive MOFs operating under mild conditions (ambient temperature and humidity).
In 2012, Sadakiyo and coworkers tried to control the hydrophilicity of the ammonium counterion $\left(\mathrm{NR}_{3}\right)$ in a 2D anionic $\mathrm{MOF}\{\mathrm{NR}_{3}(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{COOH})\}[\mathrm{MCr}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]\cdot\boldsymbol{n}\mathrm{H}_{2}O$ through alkyl chain substitution in ammonium $\mathbf{\Gamma}_{\mathrm{R-MCr}},$ where ${\bf R}={\bf M}{\bf e}$ (methyl), Et (ethyl), or Bu (n-butyl), and M = Mn or Fe).174 The 2D R-MCr MOFs are constructed by a bimetallic node linked by oxalate anions forming a honeycomb cavity. The countercation species are located in the center of the cavity in a perpendicular direction to the layer. Therefore, the counterions occupy the interlayer space and the hydrophilicity of void space is affected by the cation species. In R-MCr ( $\mathbf{\dot{M}}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n},$ , Fe), the change in metal species does not affect the structure, while the cation replacement induces low symmetry in the structure. Indeed, the ${\mathrm{Bu-MnCr}},$ , Bu-FeCr, Et-MnCr, and Me-FeCr compounds indicate a different interlayer distance of 8.52, 8.49, 7.72, and $6.91\mathrm{~\mathring{A},}$ respectively, due to the bulkiness of countercations. To investigate hydrophilicity, $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ vapor sorption isotherms were recorded at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ (Figure 33). EtMnCr, and Me-FeCr have high uptake amounts at lower vapor pressure $<60\%$ RH) with an adsorption capacity in the order of Me-FeCr (5.8 per formula unit) $>\mathrm{Et-MnCr}\ (3.2)>\mathrm{Bu-FeCr}$ (1.6), Bu-MnCr. As a control experiment, an $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ vapor sorption of $(\mathrm{NBu}_{4})[\mathrm{MnCr}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]$ (abbreviated as $\mathrm{NBu}_{4}$ ) was measured because $[\mathrm{NBu}_{3}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{COOH}]^{+}$ and $\mathrm{NBu}_{4}$ have a similar size and different hydrophilicity. $\mathrm{NBU_{4}^{\ +}}$ does not show clear adsorption property, but Me-FeCr with short alkyl chains unambiguously had a high $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ capacity at low humidity and a potential high proton conductivity. Overall, the hydrophilicity is attributed to the reduced hydrophobic alkyl chain length with increased space. As can be predicted, the conductivities evaluated by impedance measurement at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ were $0.8\times$ $10^{-4}$ for Me-FeCr ( $65\%$ RH), $1\times10^{-7}$ for Et-MnCr $(65\%)$ , $2\times$ $10^{-11}$ for Bu-FeCr $(60\%)$ , and $0.8\times10^{-11}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for ${\mathrm{Bu-MnCr}}$ $(60\%)$ . In the same conditions, the hydrophilic compound has a good conductivity in good agreement with the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ vapor uptake amounts.
In 2018, Dincă group studied the proton conductivity of MOF (MIT-25) with two distinct hydrophilic pores.175 A mesoporous MOF, MIT-25 $\begin{array}{r}{\big(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}\big(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}\big)\big(\mathrm{TTFTB}\big)_{3}.}\end{array}$ , $\mathrm{TTFTB^{4-}\ =}$ tetrathiaful-valene-tetrabenzoate),176 has two different sizes and environments of cylindrical pores with the larger pore $(27\textup{\AA})$ decorated with two $\mu_{2}\mathrm{-H}$ -bridged carboxylic acids per $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mg}}^{2+}$ and a small pore $(4.5\mathrm{~\AA~})$ with an additional pair of $\mu_{2}$ -H-bridged carboxylates directed into the pore site (Figure 34). In addition, the counter oxonium ions occupy a small pore. Therefore, the small pore is an ideal environment for Grotthuss proton conduction. The water vapor sorption isotherm clearly reflects two distinct pore properties with a two-steps adsorption profile (Figure 34b). Although the small channel is occupied by ${\mathrm{H}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}^{+}.$ , it adsorbs $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules in the low-pressure region and the large pore is not saturated at all in the high-pressure region, which is thought to be structural swelling. DFT calculations support that $7.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules can be encapsulated within the small pore despite the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}^{+}.$ , corresponding with experimental data with approximately $\mathrm{5\mmol_{H_{2}O}/m o l_{M O F}}.$ The proton conductivity of the pelletized sample was $1.58\times10^{-5}\mathrm{S}$ $\bar{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $40\%$ RH and increased to $1.03\times10^{-4}\mathrm{{Scm^{-1}}}$ $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}=0.36\mathrm{eV}\right)$ at $75^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . At $95\%$ RH, the conductivity was $6.8\times$ $10^{-5}\mathrm{{Scm}^{-1}}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and is $5.1\times10^{-4}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $\mathrm{\mathit{E}_{a}}=0.40\:\mathrm{eV})$ at $75^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . At low humidity, the proton diffusion by the Grotthuss mechanism $\mathrm{(<0.4eV)}$ is attributed to the small hydrophilic pore. Although it is difficult to evaluate $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ for proton diffusion in the large pore only, overall the proton conductivity with high humidity has a cooperative proton transfer via two distinct pores. The humidity-dependent proton conductivity with hydrophilicity emphasizes the importance of the aperture character.
## 4.7. Other Functional Guest Inclusions for Proton Conductivity
The SSPCs fall under two categories with different operating temperature conditions depending on the conducting medium (low-temperature hydrated and high-temperature anhydrous condition). As mentioned in the introduction, the selection of the conducting medium is an important factor for the application of interest. In particular, to have anhydrous conductivity, some researchers try to exclude $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules as the conducting medium and include protic organic molecules such as imidazole, triazole, and histamine (His) in the pore as alternatives, which have a high BP.
In addition, other functional molecules such as POMs and MOPs are incorporated into the void space of the various MOFs for proton conductivity (Figure 35).
In 2009, Hurd and coworkers reported anhydrous proton conductors by the controlled loading of 1H-1,2,4-triazole $\left(\mathrm{Tz}\right)$ molecules in $\mathrm{Na}_{3}(2,4,6$ -trihydroxy-1,3,5-benzenetrisulfonate) (named $\beta$ -PCMOF2).32 $\beta$ -PCMOF2 has a honeycomb structure with a 1D-channel (diameter $=5.65(2){-}5.91{\overset{\circ}{(}2)}{\overset{\circ}{\mathrm{A}}}$ ) surrounded by a sulfonate group, which can be a potential proton-conducting pathway. At $30\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ $\beta$ -PCMOF2 $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)_{0.5}$ has a conductivity of $5.0\times\mathrm{i}0^{-6}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and it decreases to $10^{-8}S$ $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ due to dehydration. To avoid guest release, the less volatile Tz molecules are incorporated in the pore through in situ loading, resulting in $\beta\mathrm{-PCMOF}2\mathrm{(Tz)}x$ $\mathbf{\chi}_{x}=0.3$ , 0.45, and 0.6). The $\mathrm{Tz}$ -loaded $\beta\mathrm{-}\mathrm{PCMOF}2\mathrm{(Tz)}x$ indicated better conductivity than $(<10^{-6}\ \mathrm{~S~}\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ pure PCMOF2 and $\mathrm{Tz}$ in the overall range of temperatures. At $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ Tz-loaded samples for $x=0.30$ , 0.45, and 0.60 have a conductivity of $2\times10^{=4}\:S$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $5\times10^{-4}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}.$ , and $4\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ , respectively. In addition, the origin of conductive ionic species was confirmed by AC impedance measurement under the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{D}_{2}$ environments. It shows three semicircles without the tail at low frequency, which are derived from the bulk, GB, and electrode polarization indicating nonion blocking. The conductivity under $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{D}_{2}$ clearly demonstrates the isotope effect with 1.5 times difference in bulk resistance. As the $\mathrm{Tz}$ loading amount increases, the slope of the Arrhenius plot is altered; $x=0.3$ displays Arrhenius behavior with $E_{\mathrm{a}}=0.51\ \mathrm{eV},$ whereas $x=0.45$ and $x=0.6$ have two slopes depending on the temperature range 1 $\mathit{\check{L}}_{\mathrm{a}}=1.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ at $50{-}90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $0.34~\mathrm{eV}$ at $90-150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 0.45; $1.87\ \mathrm{eV}$ at $23\mathrm{-}80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $0.56\ \mathrm{eV}$ at $80-150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 0.6). The low $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ at high temperature might be attributed to the enhancement of mobility of ion vacancy. The OCV estimated from MEA with $\beta\mathrm{-PCMOF2(Tz)_{0.45}}$ is $1.18\mathrm{V}$ at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $72\mathrm{{h}}$ . It decreases to 0.99 and 0.77 V at 120 and $140~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ due to the catastrophic fuel cross.
A 1D-alignment of imidazole molecules in two Al-based coordination polymers indicated an anhydrous proton conductivity in 2009 (Figure 36).144 Solid Im has a low conductivity $({\sim}10^{-8}\mathrm{\dot{S}c m^{-1}})^{17\dot{7}}$ at RT because the strong H-bond interaction reduces molecular mobility. In particular, the local molecular motion of a proton carrier is able to promote the proton transfer rate. Therefore, the er mobility is significant. The confined nanospace of MOFs affords the desired working place, where a carrier moves with unique motion not seen in the bulk state. $\left[\mathrm{Al}(\mu_{2}\mathrm{-OH})(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})\right]_{n}^{179^{\bullet}}(1)$ and $\left[\mathrm{Al}\left(\mu_{2}–\mathrm{OH}\right)\right.$ (1,4- $\mathrm{BDC})\]_{n}^{180}$ (2) as the host materials for Im inclusion have a pore size of approximately $8{\mathring\mathrm{~A}}$ with a different shape and surface potential due to the ligand symmetry. Both compounds have a 1D metal chain with a bridged −OH group, while (1) possesses bulky naphthalene in the ligand, which prohibits approaching the guest molecules around the metal chain. Meanwhile, the polar sites of compound (2) are exposed to the pore and the structural flexibility aids the interaction with polar sites. Therefore, the pore of $\left[\mathrm{Al}(\mu_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH})(1,4{\cdot}\mathrm{ndc})\right]_{n}$ might be more hydrophobic than that of $\left[\mathrm{Al}(\mu_{2}\mathbf{-OH})(1,4\mathbf{-B}\bar{\mathbf{DC}})\right]_{n}$ . The contents of impregnated Im are 0.6 imidazole/1 Al ion for (1) and 1.3 imidazole/1 Al ion, respectively. In TGA, (1) shows a single-step of weight loss and (2) indicates two steps of weight loss, which implies that (2) has two different interactions. The guest-free conductivity of (1) is $10^{-13}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ , and after Im inclusion $\left(\operatorname{Im}(\varnothing1)\right)$ it becomes $5.5\times10^{-8}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ at RT. It increases to $2.2\times10^{-5}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $\mathrm{\mathit{E}_{a}}=0.6~\mathrm{eV})$ . The compound $\operatorname{Im}(\varnothing2$ has a conductivity of $10^{-10}\mathrm{{Scm}^{-1}}$ at RT and improve to $1\times10^{-7}\mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $\mathit{'}E_{\mathrm{a}}=0.9\:\mathrm{eV},$ ). Although $\operatorname{Im}(\varnothing2$ has a higher Im content than that of $\operatorname{Im}(\varnothing1_{\cdot}$ , the conductivity is 2 orders of magnitude lower than that of $\operatorname{Im}(\varnothing1$ . It is thought to be a different molecular dynamic motion following a host−guest interaction. The molecular dynamics of the confined Im studied by $^2\mathrm{H}$ SSNMR is discussed in detail in the NMR section. This study showed a new approach for enhancing proton conductivity through the confinement of proton-carrier organic molecules in a hydrophobic space. In 2011, Umeyama and coworkers included histamine(His) in $\left[\mathrm{Al}(\mu_{2}–\mathrm{OH})(1,4–\mathrm{ndc})\right]_{n}$ for anhydrous proton conductivity.55 Histamine has a lower bp $(83^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ than that of Im $(89^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ with three proton donor and acceptor sites (Figure 35). The histamine molecules are incorporated in MOF by a simple heating method at $95~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with histamine and dry toluene and sequentially toluene solvent is removed by evacuation. The total histamine amount was $30\mathrm{wt}\%$ (one histamine per $\mathsf{A l}^{3+}$ ion) and there is no structural change in host MOF despite reduced porosity after histamine encapsulation. The conductivity of bulk histamine is $5.4\times10^{-11}\mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ at RT and $9.4\times10^{-6}\dot{\mathrm{S}}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at $75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under the anhydrous condition with $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ of $2.3\mathrm{\textrm{\eV}}$ . Moreover, it is impossible to measure high-temperature $(>80$ ${}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\ '$ ) conductivity of bulk histamine due to melting. However, the sample including histamine in MOFs had a conductivity of $3.0\times$ $10^{-5}\mathrm{{Scm}^{-1}}$ at RT and $1.7\times10^{-3}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ( $\mathrm{\Delta}R_{\mathrm{a}}=0.25$ eV). These values much higher than not only bulk histamine but also Im inclusion. The difference in conducting mechanism from $\mathrm{Im}\ @\mathrm{MOF}$ and bulk histamine was investigated through an NMR study. In terms of carrier molecular size, histamine (110 $\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{3},$ is two times larger than Im $(65\mathring\mathrm{A}^{3})$ . Nevertheless, a higher histamine content than Im was incorporated in the same MOF, which means a dense histamine packing, providing an efficient conducting pathway. Despite the same histamine and Im concentration, the conductivity of histamine $_{@\mathrm{MOF}}$ is larger than that of Im. These results mean that not only the carrier concentration but also the inherent properties of histamine are important. The conformational structure distinction of the confined state from the bulk (trans form)181 state might induce a distinct conductivity and a distinct $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ . In addition, DSC data supports that there is no phase transition in the confined histamine, whereas bulk histamine has one exothermic peak at
$83^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Solid-state $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectroscopy shows that histamine $_{@\mathrm{MOF}}$ has the sharp peaks at RT, which is a similar pattern with thermally activated bulk histamine. It means that the incorporated histamine exhibits isotropic behavior. Therefore, high concentration, isotropic behavior, and structural conformation contributed to the high proton conductivity in histamine $\smash{\mathcal{\ @}[\mathrm{Al}(\mu_{2}\cdot\mathrm{OH})(1,4\cdot\mathrm{ndc})]_{n}}$
In contrast to the previous studies focusing on conducting medium behavior without host−guest interaction under the anhydrous condition, Nguyen et al., studied the proton conductivity with the accommodation of histamine molecules into sulfonate-functionalized Zr-MOF, [VNU-23, $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{8}\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\big)_{8}\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SNDC}\big)_{4}\big]$ under hydration.182 VNU-23 initially includes DMA counterion as synthesized, which is removed by washing with 0.3 M $\mathrm{H}_{2}S{\mathrm O}_{4}$ . Next, histamine molecules were included by a simple soaking method in a methanol solution, and the excess of histamine in the intercrystals was removed $\left(\mathrm{His}_{8.2}\mathsf{C V N U-}23\right)$ ). In FT-IR, the stretching mode $(\nu_{\mathrm{N-H}})$ of the terminal amine group in histamine was observed at $3127~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ , which disappeared in $\mathrm{His}_{8.2}\mathrm{CVNU}-23$ exhibiting the absence of histamine on the surface or the inter crystal region. The strong interaction between histamine and the sulfonate functional group in VNU23 is identified by single-crystal analysis, indicating an immobile histamine species with a relatively close distance $(2.7<d<3.2$ Å). Although the pristine VNU-23 loses its structural long-range order after activation, its crystallinity is intact after histamine inclusion. The proton conductivities of pelletized ${\mathrm{His}}_{8.2}{\mathsf{C V N U}}.$ - 23 are $1.3\times10^{-6}\mathrm{Scm^{-1}}$ at $95^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $50\%$ RH and $1.79\times10^{-2}{\mathrm{S}}$ $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ at $95~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $85\%$ RH $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}\ =\ 0.27\ \mathrm{eV}\right)$ , respectively. Although the pristine VNU-23(DMA) and VNU-23 have the same Brønsted acid sites, their conductivity is 2 orders of magnitude lower $(\sim10^{-4})$ than that of $\mathrm{His}_{8.2}\mathrm{CVNU}{-23}$ . This can be attributed to the polarity of protonated histamine with guest $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . The high conductivity of $_\mathrm{His_{8.2}C V N U-}23$ continues at least for $120\mathrm{~h~}$ without degradation. This study successfully achieved a high proton conductivity through the proton-carrier anchoring strategy and visualized functional molecules inclusion (histamine docking) via structural analysis.
For another functional material inclusion in MOF for proton conductivity, Lee et al. introduced the MOP 3 ( $\mathtt{C u}$ paddle wheel-based cuboctahedron) into mesoporous MOF (PCN777).57 MOP 3, $\mathrm{Na_{6}H_{18}[C\dot{u}_{24}(S O^{3-}\cdot m B\hat{D}C)_{24}(G)_{24}]}_{!}$ has the sulfonate functional groups on the surface of a cuboctahedron and PCN-777 constructed by $\mathrm{{ZrOCl}_{2}{\cdot}8H_{2}O}$ with $4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}{\cdot}s{-}$ triazine-2,4,6-triyl-tribenzoic acid (TATB) has two types of cages ( $1.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ tetrahedral and $3.8\ \mathrm{nm}$ truncated tetrahedral cage). In particular, the mesopore $\left(3.8~\mathrm{nm}\right)$ of the host material is beneficial for encapsulation of MOP 3. With different levels of MOP concentrations, two samples were prepared ( $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{:}}2.7\:\mathrm{MOP}$ cage in MOF unit cell for UMOM-100-a and a 3.7 MOP cage in MOF unit cell for UMOM-100-b). As the MOP content increased, the mesopore gradually decreased, and micropore evolved in the pore size distribution plot, corresponding to the DFT slit model calculation. In addition, the sulfonated functional group of MOP changes the hydrophilicity of PCN77, which is reflected in the increased $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ vapor uptake. However, the total $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ vapor uptake does not change depending on the MOP contents. The proton conductivity was measured with pelletized samples under various humidity and temperature conditions. PCN-777 has a low conductivity of $5.69\times\dot{10}^{-8}\mathrm{{Scm}^{-1}}$ at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $90\%$ RH, while UMOM-100-a and UMOM-100-b are 4 orders of magnitude higher ( $\phantom{-}1.40\times$
$10^{-4}$ and $2.11\times10^{-4}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ , respectively) than that of PCN777 at the same conditions. As expected, the conductivity is in good correlation with the sulfonate group amounts. Furthermore, $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ value decreases as the MOP contents increase ( $\mathrm{\Delta}[0.87\mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathrm{PCN-777~>~0.78~\eV}$ for $\mathrm{UMOM-100-a\>\0.66\~eV}$ for UMOM-100-b). Nonetheless, it is still high due to the irregular arrangement of the MOP cage in the pore.
You and coworkers tried to impregnate a proton carrier of the ammonium salt $\left(\mathrm{NH_{4}^{+}B r}\right)$ directly into HKUST-1 for proton conductivity.183 To include the ammonium salt in MOF, HKUST-1 was immersed in a saturated $\mathrm{NH_{4}B r/E t O H}$ solution for 6 days. As a result, $\left(\mathrm{NH_{4}B r}\right)_{\mathrm{1.15}}@\mathrm{HKUST-1}$ was prepared and its conductivity was compared with the original compound under anhydrous and hydrated conditions. At $473\mathrm{~K},$ $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{Br}\right)_{1.15}@\mathrm{HKUST-1}$ had a conductivity of ${7.01\times10^{-8}\mathrm{~S~}}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , while HKUST-1 showed $2.13\times10^{-11}\mathrm{\dot{S}c m^{-1}}$ . In hydrated condition, the conductivity increased up to $8.99\times10^{-4}\mathrm{{\dot{S}}\ c m^{-1}}$ for $\left(\mathrm{NH_{4}B r}\right)_{\mathrm{1.15}}@\mathrm{HKUST-1}$ and $1.0\Bar{4}\times10^{-8}\mathrm{~S~}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for HKUST-1, respectively, at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and $99\%$ RH. With respect to HKUST-1, $\left(\mathrm{N}\mathrm{\bar{H}}_{4}\mathrm{Br}\right)_{1.15}@\mathrm{HKUST-1}$ has a high carrier density, which is strongly correlated to the high conductivity. However, $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ at $99\%$ RH for $\left(\mathrm{NH_{4}B r}\right)_{1.15}@\mathrm{HKUST-1}\left(1.42\right)$ $(1.42\ \mathrm{eV})$ is higher than that of HKUST-1 $\left(0.69\ \mathrm{\eV}\right)$ considering as vehicular conducting mechanism.
In 2014, Gassensmith and coworkers demonstrated the proton conductivity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sensing in γ-cyclodextrin(CD) MOF (CDMOF-2).184 CDMOF-2 has a cavity with $17\mathrm{~\AA~}$ diameter void and two kinds of windows (7.8 and $4.2\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ). In addition, a high density of free hydroxy group in the CD ligand can react with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reversibly forming alkyl carbonic acid (CA). The formation of carbonate by $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was confirmed by $^{13}{\mathrm{CNMR}}$ spectroscopy. On the basis of the acidic species (CA), CACDMOF-2 is anticipated to have a higher conductivity than pristine CDMOF-2. However, in contrast, the pristine CDMOF-2 has a higher conductivity of $4.8\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~\bar{~S~}~c m^{-1}}$ than that of CA-CDMOF-2 $\left(9\times10^{-9}\dot{\mathrm{S}}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\right),$ ). It was thought to be loss of base (−OH group) and the blockage of a hydrophilic window by the carbonate group resulting in lower conductivity. Nonetheless, the reversible formation of carbonate recovers the conductivity after removal of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ without degradation in performance. The dried condition improves the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sensitivity. As the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ concentration increases, the conductivity shows an exponential drop (Figure 37). At a low $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ concentration regime, the sensitivity is relatively high, which means that carboxylation reaction rate is faster than the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ diffusion rate. This research describes the application of conductivity as a gas sensor and provides a potential possibility for CA inclusion for proton-conductive MOFs.
The conductivity data of proton-conductive MOFs is summarized in Table 1. Although we could not include all the data of proton-conductive MOFs in Table 1, the logarithm of proton conductivity versus activation energy $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}\right)$ for protonconductive MOFs with different functional groups is plotted in Figure 38. In fact, several factors such as temperature, humidity, functional group, carrier density, and structure (pore) dimensionality and so on, can affect conductivity simultaneously, and the data reported do not provide the same criteria for conductivity measurement. Therefore, the plotting was simplified with the best conductivity value of each material and $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ . The distribution plot of the conductivity and $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ seems to be scattered, but there are some points that can be detected from the plot. At first, the highest conductivity with the lowest $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ among the proton-conductive MOFs seems to be achieved by the sulfonate group (section A). Second, the phosphonate functionalized group (section C) has a widely spread $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ among the functional groups in proton-conductive MOFs. Furthermore, the MOFs encapsulating counterion (section B) is belonging to relatively high conductivity $\left(>10^{-4}\thinspace\mathrm{S~cm}^{-1}\right)$ .
In this section, we discussed the various strategies from framework modification to functional guest inclusion for proton-conductive MOFs in depth. In particular, some of the methods are highly effective and promising for high performance in proton conductivity. To date, although most of the studies in proton-conductive MOFs focus on a high conductivity value, proving the conducting mechanism and providing a new alternative conducting medium to achieve practical proton conducting materials is an important for future endeavors.
## 5. THEORETICAL/COMPUTATIONAL STUDY FOR PROTON-CONDUCTION MECHANISM
A computational study aids the intuitive understanding of experimental results for various materials. In the MOF field, big data analysis for mining a potential material for the application of interest such as gas storage, structure simulation, or catalytic properties has been extensively studied using the deposited structural data.221,222 Nevertheless, the microscopic elucidation of mobile protons in the confined conducting media in protonconductive MOFs is challenging. In contrast to the huge number of experimental observations, there are a few examples of computational study in proton-conductive MOFs to provide a fundamental insight into the conduction mechanism. In this section, we deal with the MD simulation for a fundamental understanding of the proton diffusion mechanism in MOFs.
In 2013, Paesani studied the microscopic mechanism correlated with water-mediated proton diffusion in flexible MIL-53(Cr) with MD simulations as a function of temperature (300, 350, and $400~\mathrm{K}$ ), water loading $\left(N=0{-}20\operatorname{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ per unit cell) and pore size (large and narrow pore).223 For simulation, the anharmonic multistate empirical valence bond (aMS$\mathrm{EVB}\big)^{224}$ model provides the hydration structure and the charge defect related to the excess proton. One excess proton was added to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ for initial $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}^{+}$ with equilibrium time (1 ns). In $N_{\mathrm{H_{2}O}}<$ 10 loadings (narrow pore), the two featured peaks (3.0 and 5.5 Å) are observed in radial distribution function (RDF) exhibiting the interaction between the center of excess charge (CEC) and the bridge hydroxy $\left(\mu_{2}{\mathrm{-OH}}\right)$ of the metal center. However, these peaks disappear at high $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ loading (large pore) due to screening by preferential solvation. The energy for proton transport was analyzed by a potential of mean force (PMFs) calculation profile depending on the $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ loading at $300\mathrm{K}.$ . This result clearly supports the strong interaction with $\mu_{2}{\mathrm{-OH}}$ at low loading amounts, whereas proton transport is barrierless in the channel at $N_{\mathrm{H_{2}O}}>10$ . The average hydrated proton structure determined by the 2D probability distribution $P\ (\delta,\ \mathrm{Ro}_{0}\mathbf{0}_{1};$ where $\delta$ is the distance difference between CEC and the adjacent oxygen atom of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ) is a distorted Eigen-like cation225 as $N_{\mathrm{H_{2}O}}$ increase $\left(N_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}>14\right)$ . By contrast, a Zundel-like structure is observed at low $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ loading with a narrow pore state. The transition from a Zundel-like to an Eigen-like structure in MIL$53(\mathrm{Cr})$ is correlated to the breathing behavior of the framework due to the spatial restriction. The transition free energy for proton transport between two neighboring water molecules estimated from aMS-EVB3 indicated that the free energy barrier of the confined $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in the channel is lower than that of the bulk state, whereas the $N_{\mathrm{H_{2}O}}=10$ loadings show a level similar to bulk due to pore transition from narrow to large. Despite the low energy barrier, the slow proton diffusion predicted from aMSEVB3 is attributed to the slow rearrangement of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in the pore due to the guest−host interaction. The self-diffusion and reorientation are proportional to temperature and the high diffusion coefficient $(D_{\mathrm{CEC}})$ at $N_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}=7$ loadings is responsible for proton hopping by the Grotthuss mechanism inside the limited pore. Meanwhile, high diffusion coefficients $(D_{\mathrm{CEC}})$ at $N_{\mathrm{H_{2}O}}=14$ and 20 are thought to be a significant vehicle component in the large pore. In addition, the lifetime $(\tau)$ for confined water in the channel is longer than in bulk water $\dot{\tau}_{\mathrm{pc}}=$ 1.7 ps),226 which means a suppressed long-distance proton diffusion in confined $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in the pore (Figure 39). Therefore, the overall low conductivity of confined $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in MIL- ${\cdot}53(\mathrm{Cr})$ can be explained by pore change, hydrated structure, and slow rearrangement of H-bond with the interaction between the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and framework.
In 2014, Eisbein and coworkers reported a theoretical study for the proton transport in Im encapsulation in MIL-53(Al) through Born−Oppenheimer molecular dynamics (BOMD) simulations based on the density functional tight-binding (DFTB) method.227 This method is suitable for large system calculations. The guest−framework interaction was set up as three types. (1) Set 1, the quantum-mechanical/molecularmechanics (QM/MM) approach with zero charges on the MOF atoms (2) Set 2, the QM/MM approach with nonzero charges (3) Set 3, a fully quantum-mechanical (QM) treatment with the DFTB method. From the three sets of calculation for the spatial distribution and Im alignment, the Im molecules are located at the center of the pore, where neutral and protonated molecules $\left(\mathrm{H}{\cdot}\mathrm{Im}^{+}\right)$ are mixed in sets (1) and (2). However, the QM treatment [set (3)] indicated two separated species in the center of the pore where a neutral Im is located in the center and a protonated one is in the corner of a pore. This is attributed to the interaction between the $\mathrm{H}{\cdot}\mathrm{Im}^{+}$ and the carboxylate in the ligand. These results are reflected in molecular alignment in the pore. In set (1), Im molecules align along the pore direction with the Hbond between the guests. However, $\mathrm{H}{\cdot}\mathrm{Im}^{+}$ in set (3) has a preferred orientation to carboxylate exhibiting a perpendicular alignment to pore direction. These results show a good correlation with proton conductivity and the H-bond in a hydrophobic and hydrophilic channel of the previous report.144 Without interaction with the framework, an excess proton is shared by two Im molecules acting as Zundel cation-like character. In set (3), the proton transfer from $\mathrm{H}{\cdot}\mathrm{Im}^{+}$ to the carboxylate of the ligand is not observed and the carboxylate group trapped the $\mathrm{H}{\cdot}\mathrm{Im}^{+}$ prohibiting the proton-hopping diffusion. The mobility evaluated from calculating mean-square displacements of their centers of mass indicates a mobile neutral Im and a less mobile $\mathrm{H}{\cdot}\mathrm{Im}^{+}$ due to the strong H-bond. Nonetheless, proton hopping increases in excess of the system in the spatial confinement. In the static monitoring of the charge and geometry, the charge is different in all Zundel-like systems, whereas geometry is only affected around the transferred proton. Therefore, the guest/host interaction reduces the molecular motion and induces proton hopping with trapping. Without interaction, a Zundel-like cation is preferred with no trapping effect.
Borges et al. reported an experiment/simulation study using QENS and MD simulation for proton conductivity in UiO$66(\mathrm{Zr}){\cdot}(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{H})_{2}$ .206,228 $\mathrm{\cdot_{UiO-66}(\bar{Z}r)\mathrm{-(CO_{2}H)_{2}}}$ has two types of cage (tetrahedral $(5.2\mathring\mathrm{A})$ and octahedral $(7.0\mathring\mathrm{A})$ ) connected by triangular windows and indicating a high conductivity of $2.3\times$ $10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at $90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $95\%$ RH. In the fully hydrated condition, QENS analysis of $\mathrm{JiO-}66(\mathrm{Zr})\mathrm{-}(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{H})_{2}$ at low and high $\boldsymbol{Q}$ with VT $\left(300-373\mathrm{K}\right)$ provides that proton diffusion is derived from the local reorientational motion without oxygen diffusion of free proton and rotating water. The self-diffusion coefficients $\left(D_{s}\right)$ of the proton and water diffusion coefficient at $373\mathrm{K}$ are $3.5\times10^{-9}\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ $(E_{\mathrm{a}}=0.15\mathrm{eV})$ and $1.4\times10^{-10}\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}=0.20\mathrm{eV}\right)$ , which are slightly lower than those of bulk water $\left(\tilde{D_{s}}\left(\mathrm{H^{+}}\right)9.4\times10^{-9}\mathrm{m^{2}}s^{-1}\right.$ and $\mathsf{D}_{s}\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)=2.3\times10^{-9}\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ at $300~\mathrm{K}$ ).229,230 The MD simulation for $D_{s}$ of $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)$ and $\left(\mathrm{H}^{+}\right)$ using aMS-EVB model shows the similar $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ and a faster $\mathrm{H}^{+}$ diffusion than $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ corresponding to the experimental result. The relaxation time from the simulation at $300~\mathrm{K}$ has a long decay of around 270 ps (bulk $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}~=~1.7~\mathrm{ps})$ , implying a restricted proton dynamics in MOF. Furthermore, the MD simulation trajectories show that most of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules are positioned in the tetrahedral cage and a few are in the octahedral cage at $300\mathrm{~K~}$ due to the orientation of the $-\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{H}$ functional groups, making it inaccessible to octahedral cages. The strong interaction with the dangling −COOH group limits the molecular motion. However, at high temperature the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules are dispersed equally between two cages with the reduced total number of H-bond per molecules.
This state provides a long-range order of H-bonds between the tetrahedral and octahedral cages facilitating proton diffusion. This is a good example of proton conductivity supported by theoretical calculation. A further MD simulation study on UiO$66(\mathrm{Zr}){\cdot}(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{H})_{2}$ was performed by water loading dependence.228 As the $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ loading amount increases, the self-diffusion coefficient of $D_{s}\big(\mathrm{CEC}\big)$ increases and $D_{s}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ decreases because the increased number of guests spatially interrupt the molecular diffusion between cages (Figure 40b). It means that the protons predominantly diffuse by the Grotthuss mechanism. The RDF shows a strong peak at $2.{\dot{5}}-2.6{\dot{\mathrm{A}}}$ indicating a Zudeltype cation $(2.5\mathring\mathrm{A})$ . The first solvation state has insufficient Hbonds to form the Eigen cation due to space limitation. As a result, the ${\mathrm{H}}_{7}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}{}^{+}$ cation is in a stable state and proton diffusion happens between the ${\mathrm{H}}_{7}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}{}^{+}$ cation and Zundel. The decrease followed by an increase in H-bond lifetime with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ loading increase, is attributed to interaction change and steric hindrance between the guest and the host. Finally, the excess proton in the high loading of $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ effectively migrates long-distance across the three tetrahedral cages including an octahedral cage.
Most recently, Wahiduzzaman et al. studied a protonconducting mechanism in Ti-based MOF $(\mathrm{MIP-177-SO_{4}H}.$ LT) by ab initio MD (AIMD) simulation. $^{203}\mathrm{~MIP-}177{\cdot}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\mathrm{\bar{H}}.$ LT $\mathrm{\small{[Ti_{12}O_{15}(m d i p)_{3}(f o r m a t e)_{3}(S O_{4}H)_{3}}};$ mdip = $_{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ - tetracarboxydiphenylmethane] was prepared by ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}S{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ treatment of MIP-177-LT compound. Part of the formates was replaced by the hydrogen sulfate group generating Brønsted acid sites without a structure change and porosity reduction. At $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and high humidity $(95\%\ \mathrm{RH})$ , it represents a outstanding conductivity of $2.6~\times~10^{-2}~\mathrm{~S~}~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . To understand the conducting mechanism on the atomic scale, the model structure of MIP- $177{\cdot}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\mathrm{H}{\cdot}\mathrm{LT}$ assumed that the $\mathrm{\Delta}S O_{4}H$ moiety bridges the $\mathrm{Ti}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{15}$ oxo cluster. The AIMD simulation was performed at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ with a fully hydrated state. At first, H-bond formation between $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $-S\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{H}$ was evidenced by a peak at $2.7\mathring\mathrm{A}$ in RDF. In the next step, the proton donated from $-S\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{H}$ into nearby $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ stays for 21 fs, and then moves to a next $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ forming a Zundel cation. This result is in good agreement with the FT-IR study. Therefore, the computational results indicate the Grotthuss type of proton diffusion by initiated proton donation from sulfate and subsequent transport through the Hbond networks.
As discussed above, a theoretical and computational study is a powerful tool for providing an insight into the atomic scale for the proton-conduction mechanism. In particular, the crystallinity of MOFs is advantageous for designing a model for simulation. Nevertheless, it is complicated and challenging due to the many factors to consider. Therefore, for mining new proton-conductive MOFs, computational simulation for the explanation of present proton conduction as well as expectation for potential materials is a prerequisite and further study is required.
## 6. METHODS FOR THE CHARACTERIZATION OF PROTON DIFFUSION AND DYNAMICS
Conductivity measurement is the most fundamental and critical evaluation for proton-conductive materials, and electrical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) is a basic technique for this. In addition, single-crystal X-ray analysis, solid-state NMR spectroscopy, and QENS are important tools for supporting the proton motion, conducting pathway and mechanism in the materials. In this section, we deal with the technical factors and pros and cons of the characterization tools for protonconductive materials.
## 6.1. IS Measurement
In general, IS is a fundamental tool for evaluating the conductivity of materials. Since the first introduction of electrical impedance by Oliver Heaviside in the 1880s, impedance has become a general concept of resistance due to the considering phase difference. In particular, IS is a new and powerful tool for understanding the electrical properties of materials and interfaces. The common approach without a modifier is to apply the frequency domain and to measures the monochromatic signal of the vector sum expressed by the real and imaginary part of the resulting current, exhibiting a full semicircular arc $(\mathrm{Z}=a+j b,j\equiv\sqrt{-1})$ . The resulting impedance $\scriptstyle\left(Z(\omega)=Z^{\prime}+j Z^{\prime\prime}\right)$ (frequency dependent) can be plotted in a complex plan with rectangular or polar coordination (eq 6). Sometimes the complex plan plots also called Nyqist plots (diagrams).
$$
\operatorname{Re}(Z)\equiv Z^{\prime}=|Z|\mathrm{cos}(\theta)\mathrm{and}\operatorname{Im}(Z)\equiv Z^{\prime\prime}=|Z|\mathrm{sin}(\theta)
$$
Commercial instruments are available, and the automatic measurement system as a function of frequency with various frequencies from $1\mathrm{mHz}$ to $1\mathrm{MHz}$ is advantageous for the user and provides a better signal-to-noise ratio. The disadvantages of IS basically lie in the possible ambiguities in interpretation. The critical problem in the analysis is that ideal circuit elements inadequately demonstrate the electrical response with a lumped constant. Thus, it is often found that $\mathbf{Z}(\omega)$ cannot be evaluated by an equivalent circuit. In this case, the use of distributed impedance elements [e.g., constant-phase elements (CPEs)] in the equivalent circuit plays an important role in the process of fitting the observed impedance data. Furthermore, in terms of impedance value, the low-Impedance materials should be measured using a 4-terminal technique (four-probe method) to remove the parasitic contact resistance, which can significantly affect sample measurement, whereas for high impedance materials it is enough to use a 2-terminal connection (two-probe method) because the impedance is higher than that of test cables.
Typically, the impedance of the proton-conductive MOFs is evaluated under various conditions such as pressure, temperature, and humidity. Thus, the possible behavior such as charge carrier blocking and nonblocking at the interface between the material and the electrode should be discussed according to the measurement condition, which is reflected in the tail of the Nyquist plot at low frequency. In addition, most of the measurements of proton-conductive MOFs are performed using a pellet. As a result, the GB effect reflected in the Nyquist plot as multiple semicircles can be discussed Although a large number of proton-conductive MOFs studies reported conductivity values from the pellets, here, we focus on a handful examples of single-crystal impedance measurement in detail.
6.1.1. Impedance for Single Crystal Samples. One of the best ways to understand the inherent conductivity and conducting pathways of materials is single-crystal conductivity measurement with single-crystal structure analysis. In particular, single-crystal to single-crystal (SC-SC) transformation caused by proton transport is highly desirable understanding protonconducting pathway, but this field has not yet been studied. Compared with microcrystalline materials (pelletized sample measurements), direct single-crystal conductivity measurement not only excludes GB effect but also observe the anisotropic conductivity through the dependence on direction. Nevertheless, single-crystal measurement in MOFs is challenging because it requires a suitable crystal size (large) with welloriented shape, sufficient stability with high crystallinity at elevated temperatures, and harsh sample-contact preparation. Therefore, the conductivity in most previously reported protonconductive MOFs was recorded for pelletize samples with the two-probe method. In this section, we deal with representative proton-conductive MOFs exhibiting single-crystal conductivity and how to understand the proton-conducting pathway.
In 2014, Bao and coworkers reported single-crystal conductivity of 2D $\mathrm{\bigl[Co^{III}L a^{I I I}(n o t p H)(H_{2}O)_{6}\bigr]C\bar{l}O_{4}{\cdot}}5\dot{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ (CoLa-II), which undergoes an SC-SC transformation above $45~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $93\%$ RH.191 CoLa-II has a protruded phosphonate group and coordinated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ toward interlayers and lattice $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ with $\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}$ between the positive layers (interlayer distance $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ $15.94\mathring{\mathrm{A}},$ ). The layers extend to the ac-plane with stacking along the $b$ -axis. The solid-state phase transition of CoLa-II above 45 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $93\%$ RH results in $\mathrm{\bar{[H_{3}O]}[C o L a\cdot\bar{(n o t p)}(H_{2}O)_{4}]C l O_{4}}.$ $3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{i}}$ , (CoLa-III), which has a similar layer structure with CoLa-I,231 but with different interlayer contents. Hence, in the dehydration and rehydration process, CoLa-II is irreversible, where the PXRD pattern of rehydrated CoLa-II is similar to the CoLa-I previously reported. The conductivity of CoLa-II using a pelletized sample was $3.50\times10^{-6}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $95\%$ RH and decreased to $6.46\times10^{-9}\mathrm{{S}\mathrm{{cm}^{-1};}}$ at $40\%$ RH. The humiditydependent conductivity supports the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules in the interlayer space of the conducting pathway. A large single crystal of CoLa-II was available for conductivity measurements and the direction of the crystal was determined by XRD. The conductivity along the $a$ -axis was $3.05\times10^{-4}\mathrm{~S~}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}~95\%$ RH, which is 2 orders of magnitude higher than the pellet. Based on the conductivity data of $15{-}30^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the activation energies for proton transport in CoLa-II are 0.34 and $0.42\mathrm{eV}$ for pellet and single crystal, respectively. Compared with the pellet sample, the conductivity deviation for single crystal depending on the temperature is not significant before and after phase transition due to the intact conducting pathway. One year later, the same authors reported on the compound ${\mathrm{CoCa}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{nH}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}} $ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{III}}{\mathrm{Ca}}^{\mathrm{II}}{\left(\mathrm{notpH}_{2}\right)}{\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\hat{\mathrm{O}}\right)}_{2}{\mathrm{J}}{\mathrm{ClO}}_{4}{\cdot}{\mathrm{nH}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{5}$ , in which Ln was replaced with the ${\mathrm{Ca}^{\mathrm{II}}}$ ion, resulting in a different topology.
${\mathrm{CoCa}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{nH}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}$ has a similar positive layer structure with a different interlayer distance of $12.3\bar{8}7\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ . Unlike the Ln compound, CoCa· $\mathrm{\nH}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ has a reversible phase transition with dehydration ( $\mathsf{\tilde{C}o C a}$ $2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ at $40\%$ RH) and hydration $\mathrm{(CoCa\cdot4H_{2}O}$ at $95\%$ RH) state depending on the RH. The phase transition is strongly correlated with $2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ as well as $\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}$ , which significantly affects the H-bond network in the AB-plane of interlayer space. In particular, $\mathrm{CoCa}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ indicates a good 1D H-bonding chain between $\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}$ and lattice $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ along the $b$ -axis with successive H-bonds with phosphonate oxygen. Meanwhile, in $\mathrm{CoCa}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , the H-bonds are localized between $\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}$ and lattice $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ without extension. Hence, the effective proton-conduction pathway might be blocked, resulting in reduced conductivity. The pelletized sample shows a conductivity of $1.55\times10^{-5}\mathrm{~\textbar{~}{~S~}~}$ $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $95\%$ RH, which is one order higher than that of the Ln compound. At low humidity ( $40\%$ RH, $\mathrm{CoCa}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ state), the conductivity was $0.79\times10^{-10}\mathrm{Scm^{-1}}$ . To confirm the anisotropic conductivity, single-crystal conductivity was measured along the [010], [202], and [201̅] directions, respectively. As expected, [010] had the highest conductivity value of $1.00\times$ $10^{-3}\dot{\mathrm{S}}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $95\%$ RH that is 2 orders of magnitude higher than that of the pellet. The lowest conductivity value originated from the $[20-\bar{1}]$ direction with $4.35\times10^{-8}\mathrm{{Scm}^{-1}}$ at the same condition. The [202] direction represents the middle of the conductivity value $(4.49\times10^{-6}\mathrm{S}\mathrm{cm}^{\hat{-}_{1}})$ despite the weak H-bond networks featured. These results clearly demonstrated the proton conductivity from the highly oriented H-bond in a single crystal.
In 2017, Shimizu group reported high proton conductivity at low humidity in sulfonated In MOF (PCMOF-17) with a n i s o t r o p i c c o n d u c t i v i t y . 1 9 8 P C M O F - 1 7 [ $\operatorname{In}\left(5-\right.$ $\mathrm{Hsip})_{2}(\mathrm{M}\mathrm{\bar{e}_{2}N H_{2}})\mathrm{]}{\cdot}\mathrm{DMF}{\cdot}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{1.4};$ $\left(5{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{sip}\ =\ 5\right.$ -sulfoisophthalic acid) is a 2D anionic-layered structure incorporating $\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+}$ . The countercation and lattice $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ form a complicated H-bond network along the [001] plane. In the FT-IR spectrum, the characteristic peaks of both $-S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ and $-S{\mathrm{O}}_{3}^{-}$ groups are observed due to the dynamic proton. The proton conductivity of PCMOF-17 in powder form is $1.17\times$ $\mathsf{\widehat{10}}^{-3}\mathsf{S}\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}=0.31~\mathrm{eV}\right)$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $40\%$ RH, which is comparable to Nafion 117 $\mathrm{{(1.4\times10^{-3}S c m^{-1}}}$ at $31^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and RH $40\%$ ).232 Notably, PCMOF-17 presents a modest stability under humidity. The framework starts to decompose at a moderately high RH $(60\%)$ . Thus, single-crystal conductivity measurement is needed to avoid the argument regarding the origin of conductivity. The single-crystal conductivity was measured along the triaxial plane [1̅10], [110], and [001]. The conductivity values of $1.25\times10^{-3}\:$ , $1.20\times10^{-3}$ , and $8.66\times$ $10^{-5}~\mathrm{~S~}\mathrm{~cm}^{-1}$ were observed in [1̅10], [110], and [001] crystallographic facets, respectively. Both [1̅10] and [110] faces are crystallographically equivalent where successive Hbond networks are present. Consequently, these faces predominantly contribute to the bulk state conductivity (1.17 $\mathrm{\dot{\times}10^{-3}S c m^{-\mathrm{\dot{1}}})}$ . These results clearly proved that dissolution at GB was not the origin for high proton conductivity $(>10^{-3}\ S$ $\mathsf{c m}^{-1)}$ of the bulk state.
