## 6.2. SSNMR Spectroscopy
The proton-conduction mechanism within MOFs is postulated by using a combination of X-ray structure analysis and $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ from the VT impedance measurements. Even though it is a general method for the conduction mechanism driven by either Grotthuss or vehicle mechanism, these tools do not provide a concrete explanation for the dynamic motion of proton conduction. In particular, a light element like hydrogen with a low electron density is hardly detectable by X-rays, and NMR is a more effective characterization method in this case for understanding the H-bond interaction with the environment surrounding the proton. The dynamic behavior of protons can be studied by NMR measurements through the analysis of line shape or spin-relaxation time at variable temperature. Furthermore, pulse field gradient (PFG)-NMR gives a quantitative analysis for the mobile proton transport rate with a diffusion coefficient indicating the attenuated signal intensity, although it cannot be used to distinguish between conducting mechanisms (Grotthuss or vehicle). Therefore, the SSNMR measurement provides fundamental and critical information about conducting media, but also about the interaction between the conducting media and frameworks in a confined environment. In this section, we discuss the mobile proton characterization tools.
6.2.1. $^2{\mathsf{H}}$ NMR. The dynamic motion of the proton in proton conductors can be featured by the time scale and geometry of molecular dynamics. $^2\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectroscopy has been widely used in various materials to characterize molecular dynamic.109 As the motion freezes, the signal indicates a Pake pattern with quadrupolar splitting, whereas line shape and amplitude are affected by geometry and the rate of dynamics in the presence of motion. As the rate of molecular motion is similar to the strength of the quadrupolar interaction, the intensity of signal decreases due to the destructive interferences in the refocusing process. In fast motion, all interactions become average, exhibiting the pattern depending on the motion geometry. The simple dynamic models are summarized in Figure 42, where the fast motion model indicates the same isotropic signal in a different model with loss of the anisotropic signal. It means that fast dynamic motion is not sensitive to geometry. However, slow motion at low temperature can be defined as a specific model with a different line shape.
In 2010, Jared et al. described the molecular dynamics in a layered phosphonate MOF (PCMOF-3, $\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{L})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}$ · $2\mathrm{\dot{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}\mathrm{(L\^{\sim}}=\ \mathrm{\hat{[}}1,3,5\$ -benzenetriphosphonate] $]^{6-}$ ) with a $^2\mathrm{H}$ SSNMR study. In the structure, the layered PCMOF-3 displays an interlayer distance of 11.486(1) Å and phosphonate oxygen atoms protrude into the interlayer space. In this space, lattice $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules are anchored with phosphonate oxygen through the H-bond, which is defined by a single-crystal $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray analysis. For $^2\mathrm{H}$ SSNMR, the samples were prepared in $\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and VTSSNMR was measured. At $77\mathrm{K},$ the static $^2\mathrm{H}$ signal appears as a broad Pake pattern. With increase in temperature, the two signals of the broad peak at $77\mathrm{K}$ and another narrow line overlap by $240~\mathrm{K}$ showing dynamic averaging. The vanished broad line by $250~\mathrm{K}$ with presence of line shape means that all of the $^2\mathrm{H}$ atoms were dynamically equivalent. The signal implies a rapid exchange of $^{2}\mathrm{{H}}$ involving reorientation and translation within the 2D layers. However, it is difficult to define whether the species are $\mathrm{^{2}H^{+}}$ or $\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ for the origin of motion. After than, the simple line shape of an isotropic signal is observed until RT with further exchange process among crystallites with extra $\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules.
As previously mentioned in the section on guest inclusions, the molecular dynamics of the confined Im in of $[\mathrm{Al}(\mu_{2}-$ $\operatorname{OH})(1,4{\mathrm{-ndc}})]_{n}$ (1) and $\left[\mathrm{Al}(\mu_{2}\mathrm{-OH})(1,4\mathrm{-BDC})\right]_{n}$ (2) was demonstrated by a temperature-dependent $^2\mathrm{H}$ SSNMR study for intuitive insight into proton conduction (Figure 43).144 Both compounds display a Pake-type pattern of the anisotropic signal with a splitting width of $120~\mathrm{\kHz}$ at RT. However, the Lorentzian type of the isotropic signal for mobile Im appears at the center of the Pake pattern from $20~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{i}}$ and signal intensity increases as the temperature increases. In contrast to $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Im}}({\widehat{\boldsymbol{\omega}}}2,$ Im in 1 is more mobile at RT and has a predominant isotropic signal at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ without a Pake-doublet pattern, implying that Im has a fast isotropic motion in the pore. Consequently, it could be concluded that the degree of motional behavior of Im confined in 1 is greater than that of 2, resulting in a difference in conductivity.
In 2018, Pili et al. reported proton conductivity on the proton conductivity in nonporous MOFs (MFM-550(M), $[\bf M(\hat{H}_{4}L^{1})]_{:}$ , 1 $\mathrm{\Delta\H_{4}L^{1}=}$ biphenyl- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -diphosphonic acid; $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{L}\mathbf{a}$ , Ce, Nd, $\mathrm{Sm},$ Gd, Ho), MFM-550(Ba), $\left[\mathrm{\bf\bar{B}a}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}^{1})\right]$ , and MFM-555(M), $\begin{array}{r}{\big[\mathbf{M}\big(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}^{2}\big)\big],}\end{array}$ , $\mathrm{\mathrm{~\stackrel{~}{H}}}_{4}\mathrm{L}^{2}=$ benzene-1,4-diphosphonic acid; $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{La}$ , Ce, Nd, $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Sm}}$ , Gd, Ho) with $^2\mathrm{{H}}$ SSNMR and neutron spectroscopy studies.234 These materials show an enhanced conductivity by substitution of a metal ion $\left(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\right)$ with a low oxidation state $\left(\mathtt{B a}^{2+}\right).$ , short organic linker, and introduction of additional acidic protons $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{\dot{S}O}_{4}\mathrm{)}$ . The isostructural crystal structures of Ln MOF were solved by Rietveld refinement. The proton conductivity of MFM- ${\mathfrak{s s o}}({\mathrm{Ln}})$ is ${\sim}10^{-6}\mathrm{Scm^{-1}}$ at $20{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $99\%$ RH. To improve conductivity, at first, $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ metal ion is replaced with $\mathrm{Ba}^{2+}$ for an additional proton to compensate for the loss of the positive charge. It indicates 1 order of magnitude increase at the same condition. Second, the ligand is replaced by a short ligand (MFM-550 $\left(\operatorname{Ln}\right)$ to MFM-555 $\left(\mathrm{L}\mathrm{\bar{n}}\right)$ ). This reduces the distance between the metal phosphonate chains resulting in increased proton concentration in a unit volume. Consequently, the conductivity improved by 2 orders of magnitude $\left(\sim10^{-4}\mathrm{~\s~}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\right)$ . As the last strategy, MFM$55\bar{5}(\mathrm{Ho})$ is treated with 2 M ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}.$ . This is most effective, increasing the conductivity up to $\stackrel{\cdot}{\sim}10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The $^2\mathrm{H}$ SSNMR was performed on deuterated $\left[d\mathbf{\cdotMFM-}555(\mathbf{Ho})\right]$ at different hydration levels (dehydrated, partially hydrate, and saturated) and temperatures (between 123 and $403\mathrm{~K})$ for proton dynamics. Due to the low porosity $(<1\mathring\mathrm{~A~})$ of MFM
6.2.2. PFG-NMR. In 1965, Stejskal and Tanner demonstrated the first experimental and theoretical self-diffusion coefficient through an NMR measurement.235 The diffusion signal by molecular displacement is measured by PFG-NMR with exponentially attenuated intensity through PFG-based two pulse sequence, either a spin echo (SE) or a stimulated echo (STE) (Figure 44). PFG-NMR can be applied not only in a simple system (free molecular diffusion) but also in a complicated system (restricted/anisotropic molecular displacement) of proteins, pharmaceuticals, porous solids, heterogeneous materials, and liquid crystals. As shown in Figure 44, the first step of both pulse sequences excites the spin with $90^{\circ}$ radio frequency (RF), where spins are positioned in the xy plane accompanied by transverse relaxation $\left(T_{2}\right)$ . Then, the first gradient pulse with intensity $(g)$ and duration $(\delta)$ is applied. As soon as the gradient pulse is finished, a spatial spin phase shift occurs by molecular diffusion during the time interval $(\Delta)$ . During this diffusion time, either transversal or longitudinal magnetization is observed for the SE or STE pulse sequence, respectively. After the diffusion time $\left(\Delta\right)$ , the second gradient pulse with the same $\mathbf{g}$ and $\delta$ as the first pulse is applied for a rephasing spin. Finally, the intensity of the signal is recorded by the gradient pulse area $(g$ and $\delta\dot{}$ ), the diffusion time $(\Delta)$ , as well as the diffusion coefficient $(D)$ as (eq 7).
$$
S=S_{0}\mathrm{e}^{-(\gamma g\delta)^{2}\Delta D}
$$
When molecules diffuse during $\Delta$ , the rephasing of the spin will not be perfect because the spins have moved to a different position. As a result, a decrease in the NMR signal intensity can be observed, where signal loss is directly dependent on the gradient pulse intensity $(g)$ and duration $(\delta)$ . The proton diffusion coefficient $(D)$ can be obtained from the fitting $\hat{(}S/S_{0})$ versus $-(\gamma g\delta)^{2}\Delta$ . To obtain good data, determination of pulse sequence and diffusion parameters are important. Although the choice of the pulse sequence is dependent on the samples, STE is most often used, where magnetization is relaxed by spin−lattice relaxation time (T1) that is generally longer than the spin−spin relaxation time (T2), which is governing the signal loss in SE sequences. Compared to the SE-based sequence, STE has a longer spin diffusion time. There are numerous case studies with various samples.236 Furthermore, for an accurate diffusion coefficient, the exact setting of diffusion parameters from the reference spectrum with a few percent error is important. The modification of the gradient pulse area $(g\delta)$ affects to the signal attenuation more than the diffusion time $\left(\Delta\right)$ parameter. The gradient area consists of three parameters (gradient duration $\bar{(\delta)}$ , intensity $(g)$ and shape). The gradient pulse duration used to set 1 to $2.5~\mathrm{ms}$ and the total duration of the gradient pulse is limited by the time needed to receive current in the gradient coil. The diffusion time $(\Delta)$ can be set to any value from ${\sim}10$ ms to a few seconds including the upper limit of the relaxation time T1 or T2 depending on pulse sequence (SE or STE). When the NMR signal disappears by relaxation and diffusion, the diffusion time $\bar{(\Delta)}$ should be selected to reduce the degree of signal attenuation.
In proton-conductive MOFs, Jared et al. reported on proton mobility in defective UiO-66 by STE PFG-1 NMR.58 The diffusion constant of the mobile proton for sample (6), which was modified by stearic acid, was $4.0\times10^{-7}\mathrm{{^cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{{s}^{-1}}}$ with conductivity of $6.93\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . In 2013, Horike and coworkers studied the $\mathbf{H}^{+}$ and $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ion diffusion in postsynthetically added LiCl in the Ca-based MOF, $[\mathrm{Ca}(\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{4}^{-})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]$ (1) (Figure 45).237 The Ca-based MOF having 1D rectangular channel $\langle3.4\times3.4\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ along the $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{c}}$ -axis) indicates a drastically increased ionic conductivity of over $10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{RH}\:=\:20{-40\%}$ after LiCl inorganic salt inclusion. The $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ evaluated from the temperature-dependent conductivities was of $0.18{\mathrm{~eV}}$ . Without inorganic salt, the conductivity was $5.4\times$ $10^{-9}\mathrm{{Scm}^{-1}}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{RH}=40\%$ due to strong trapping. The mobility of ionic diffusion in (1)-LiCl was directly proved by solid-state $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ and ${^{7}\mathrm{Li}}\mathrm{NMR}.$ At RT, $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ and $^{7}\mathrm{{Li}}$ NMR show a sharp peak, indicating the liquid-like fast motion. Even at $^{-20}$ $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $40\%$ RH $\left(0.1\bar{3}\mathrm{kPa}\right)$ , the conductivity was still high $(8\times$ $10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ ). From the STE-PFG-NMR, the self-diffusion coefficients $(D)$ are $6.1\times10^{-10}\mathrm{m}^{2}s^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ and $4.9\times10^{-10}\mathrm{m}^{2}$ $\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{s}}^{-1}$ for Li. The $D$ value for $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ is comparable to that of Nafion117 (D $\mathrm{\Omega^{\prime}(^{1}H)=8.0\times10^{-10}\ m^{2}s^{-1}}$ and conductivity is $1.4\times10^{-3}\mathrm{{S}}$ $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ at $31~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{RH}=40\%$ ).232 Interestingly, $D$ for $^\mathrm{7Li}$ is similar to that of ${}^{1}\mathrm{H},$ which implies that $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ also contributes to the ionic conductivity. In fact, IS technique cannot discriminate the source of origin in ionic conductivity, where the diffusion coefficient $(D)$ of each nucleus in PFG-NMR provides quantitative information with the origin of ionic diffusion.
## 6.3. QENS
Unlike the X-ray analysis technique, neutron diffraction is very important as a unique tool to investigate hydrogen, because the incoherent neutron scattering cross-section of the H atom is considerably larger ${\mathit{\sigma}}_{\mathrm{inc}}=80$ barn, where barn $=10^{-28}\mathrm{m}^{2\cdot}$ than the other atoms such as $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}}$ ( $\dot{\sigma}_{\mathrm{inc}}=0$ barn), N ( $\overset{\cdot}{\underset{\mathrm{inc}}{}}=0.5$ barn), O $(\sigma_{\mathrm{inc}}=0\mathrm{barn})$ , and D ( $\dot{\sigma}_{\mathrm{inc}}=2.1$ barn). It means that self-motion including an $\mathrm{~H~}$ atom can be selectively detected by incoherent scattering. Furthermore, QENS provides information on dynamics, resulting from a broadening of the incident neutron spectrum due to energy transfers. In 2014, Miyatsu et al. studied the correlation between proton conductivity and the phase transition and proton dynamics in 2D oxalate MOF $((\mathrm{NH_{4}})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{adp})\hat{[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{K}_{2}\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{adp}\big)\big[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\big(\mathrm{ox}\big)_{3}\big]$ · $3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ through a study using the adiabatic calorimetry and neutron scattering study in a wide temperature range $(5-300$ K).238 The adiabatic calorimetry study gave different phase transition temperatures, $86~\mathrm{K}$ for $(\mathrm{NH}_{4})_{2}^{\circ}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{adp})[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{\bar{ox}})_{3}]$ · $3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $138\mathrm{K}$ for $\mathrm{K}_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{adp})[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ exhibiting an entropy change. $(\mathrm{NH_{4}})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{adp})[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ has a drastic entropy change at the transition temperature, whereas $\mathrm{K}_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}^{-}\mathrm{adp})[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ indicated a gradual increase around the transition temperature. From the transition entropy value, the types of transition in $(\mathrm{NH}_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{adp})[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}^{-}]$ · $3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{\bar{K_{2}}(H_{2}a d p)[Z n_{2}(o x)_{3}]{\cdot}3H_{2}O}$ were decided as an order−disorder phase transition $\begin{array}{r}{\left({\cal R}\mathrm{ln}2\stackrel{-}{\approx}5.95\mathrm{J}\mathrm{K}^{-1}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\right)}\end{array}$ ) and displacive type $\left(R\mathrm{ln}2~>~3.70~\mathrm{J}~\mathrm{K}^{-1}~\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\right)$ , respectively. For neutron scattering, the frameworks of isostructural samples with a different countercation, $\mathrm{(NH_{4})_{2}(H_{2}a d p)[Z n_{2}(o x)\bar{_{3}}]\cdot3H_{2}O}$ and $\mathrm{K}_{2}\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{adp}\big)[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ were deuterated except for guest molecules $\mathrm{(NH}_{4}^{+}$ , adipic acid, and ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}\dot{\phantom{.}}$ ) to reduce the scattering strength. Therefore, all QENS signals for $\mathrm{~H~}$ atoms were assumed as standing for guest molecules $\mathrm{\left(NH_{4}^{+}\right.}$ , adipic acid, and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ). Three neutron spectrometers (AGNES, IRIS, and HFBS) having different energy resolution (time range between 0.1 ps and $10~\mathrm{{ns}}^{\cdot}$ ) were used for the neutron scattering measurements. The neutron diffraction pattern displays a shifted Bragg peak at $Q~=~1.60{-1.64}~\mathrm{A}^{-\bar{1}}$ for $(\mathrm{N}\bar{\mathrm{H}}_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{adp})$ - $\mathrm{\big[Zn_{2}\big(o x\big)_{3}\big]}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $Q\ =\ 1.67{-}1.70\ \mathrm{\A}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{K}_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{adp})$ - $\mathrm{[Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ around the transition temperature, which is not observed in X-ray. This implies that the phase transition is correlated with the hydrogen dynamics. The dynamic structure factor $[s(Q,\omega)]$ was fitted by eq 8 (Figure 46).
$$
{\cal S}(Q,\mathfrak{\omega})={\cal R}(Q,\mathfrak{\omega})\otimes\left\{\delta(\mathfrak{\omega})+\sum_{\mathrm{i}}\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{i}}(\mathrm{Q},\mathfrak{\omega})\right\}+\mathrm{BG}
$$
where $R(Q\ \omega)$ is the resolution function; $\delta(\omega)$ is a static component (slow motion); $\mathrm{L}_{\mathrm{i}}(\mathrm{Q}\omega)$ is a relaxation of hydrogen. The relaxation dynamics studied by QENS at VT measurement indicates four relaxation modes with different activation energies 1 $\left(E_{\mathrm{a}}=2.3\right)\$ 4.5, 7.7, and $12.9\ \mathrm{kJmol^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{L}_{1},\mathrm{L}_{2},\mathrm{L}_{3},$ and $\mathrm{L}_{4},$ respectively) in $(\mathrm{NH_{4}})_{2}(\mathrm{adp})[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]{\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}}\mathrm{O}$ and only two relaxation modes $\mathrm{{\L}}_{\mathrm{{K1}}}$ and ${\mathrm{L}}_{\mathrm{K}2,} $ ) in $\mathrm{K}_{2}(\mathrm{adp})[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ . The two relaxation modes of $\mathrm{K}_{2}(\mathrm{adp})[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ are coincident with $\mathrm{L}_{3},$ and $\mathrm{L}_{4}$ of $(\mathrm{NH_{4}})_{2}(\mathrm{adp})[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ox})_{3}]{\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}}\mathrm{O}_{1}$ , implying that the $\mathrm{L}_{1}$ and $\mathbf{L}_{2}$ with low $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ originated from $\mathrm{NH_{4}^{+}}$ species and the other modes are associated with guest $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ because the H-bond of $\mathrm{NH_{4}^{+}}$ is weaker than that of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . The geometrical information associated with relaxation modes of $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{4}^{+}}$ was evaluated from the integral intensity with an elastic incoherent structure factor (EISF, $\bar{A}_{0}(\boldsymbol{Q}))$ where $I_{\mathrm{elastic}}$ and $I_{\mathrm{QENS}}$ are the peak intensities of the elastic and quasi-elastic scatterings, respectively.
$$
a_{0}(Q)=\frac{I_{\mathrm{elastic}}(Q)}{I_{\mathrm{elastic}}(Q)+I_{\mathrm{QENS}}(Q)}
$$
According to the EISF fitted to the various functions of dynamic model,239 $\mathrm{NH_{4}^{+}}$ has a continuous $C_{3}$ axis rotation during the $C_{3}$ jumping motion (Figure 46d). These results unveiled the phase transition and the unique dynamic motion of $\mathrm{NH_{4}^{+}}$ for proton transport.
In 2016, the intrinsic proton diffusion in phosphonated MOFs, [MFM-500(M), $\bar{\mathrm{M}_{3}}(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{L})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{9}(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{\bar{H_{6}}}\mathrm{SO}\bar{)_{3}}]$ $\mathbf{\nabla}\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{\nabla}$ Ni, Co; ${\mathrm{H}}_{6}{\mathrm{L}}=$ benzene-1,3,5- $p$ -phenylphosphonic acid), was studied by Pili et al. using QENS.193 MFM-500 (M) is a 2Dstructure with half of deprotonation. The conductivities of MFM-500 (Ni) and MFM-500 $\scriptstyle(\mathbf{Co})$ are $4.5\times10^{-4}$ and $4.4\times$ $10^{-5}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $98\%$ RH, respectively. The different conductivity in the isostructural compounds might be attributed to the distinct bond strength between coordinated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . The conducting pathway was anticipated by the single-crystal structure analysis to be thought free phosphonic acid groups and coordinated $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules. For proton dynamics, QENS measurements for MFM-500 (Ni) were performed at varying temperatures (from $^{-23}$ to $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\$ ) under both anhydrous and
$98\%$ RH conditions. The Q-dependent EISF plot was fitted with the theoretical dynamic model, where “free diffusion inside a sphere” is the best description rather than jump diffusion. The distance $(r)$ for dependency of free diffusion inside a sphere is extracted from half-width of the half-maximum $(\mathrm{HWHM},\Gamma)$ of QENS spectra. $\Gamma$ depends on $Q^{2}$ up to $Q^{2}>1.95\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-2}$ $\stackrel{\cdot}{r}\leq2.25$ Å), which means proton diffusion within 2.25 Å. However, $\Gamma$ in $\mathring{Q^{2}}<1.95\mathring{\mathrm{~A}}^{-2}$ $\hat{\zeta}_{r}\leq2.25\mathrm{~\AA})$ is independent, indicating the confined motion, where additional $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules are required for proton conduction.
Recently, Rought et al. reported the analogous conducting mechanism by the modulation of proton diffusion with the incorporation of accessible carboxylate group in MOFs.51 To introduce the accessible carboxylate group, Ba-MOFs (MFM510 (tricarboxylic acid), $-511$ (tetracarboxylic acid), and $-512$ (5-pentacarboxylic acid)) were synthesized with multiple carboxylate functional groups in the ligand. In MFM-510, all carboxylates were coordinated to the metal center, whereas MFM-511 has monodentate carboxylates with restricted mobility by a strong H-bond with a neighboring carboxylic oxygen. Therefore, both compounds show moderate conductivity values $(2.1\times10^{-5}$ for MFM-510 and $5.1\times10^{-5}\mathrm{~S~}$ $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ for MFM-511 at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $99\%$ RH) due to the lack of accessible carboxylate. However, MFM-512 obtained one free carboxylic acid functional group, resulting in enhanced conductivity of $2.9\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}^{.}$ under the same condition. In addition, the water adsorption trend is in good agreement with the conductivities at $99\%$ RH and $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The QENS measurement for MFM-512 demonstrated the diffusional dynamics with change in the inelastic scattering around the elastic signal. It results in a broadening of the line width correlated with incoherent scattering. The $Q$ -dependent EISF plot was successfully fitted with the “free diffusion inside a sphere” model (eq 10).
$$
\mathrm{EISF}=p+(1-p)[3j_{\mathrm{1}}(\mathrm{Q_{\mathrm{r}}})/(\mathrm{Q_{\mathrm{r}}})]^{2}
$$
where $j_{1},r,p,$ and $\left(p-1\right)$ mean the first-order spherical Bessel function, radius of the sphere, and the immobile and mobile fractions in the diffusion process, respectively. From the fitting result, $r$ has a distance of 1.8995 Å coincident with the H-bond distance in single-crystal $\mathrm{x}$ -ray analysis.
Both NMR and QENS techniques verify the proton diffusion dynamics within the molecular scale, and they are complementary skills to one another in different time/space scales. QENS can focus on a shorter time scale, which means the difficulty to detect slow motion, whereas NMR can cover a much wider time scale, even slow motion. Therefore, it is important to select the appropriate tool depending on the dynamic motion of the time scale for each sample.
## 7. CONCLUSION AND PERSPECTIVES
In this Review, we discussed the progress of proton-conductive MOFs during the past decade. In terms of a materials design perspective, MOFs provide precise structural information, designability, and variable modulation methods (tunability) to promote proton transport. As introduced in the Introduction, several approaches for proton-conductive MOFs were found to achieve high performance in proton conduction. From the previous results, some general conclusions with a view to achieving proton-conductive MOFs are summarized below.
(i) Increase the proton-carrier concentration. According to the previous research in proton-conductive MOFs, one proton source for carrier concentration originates from additional guests (moisture, acids, and protic organic molecules), countercations $\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}^{+},\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}\right).$ , and ${\mathrm{Me}}_{2}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}^{+}$ ), and additional functional groups in the organic strut. Among them, ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ is the most effective candidate for high conductivity with low $E_{\mathrm{a}}$ due to its lower $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ value (1.92 for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and 2.15 for ${\mathrm{H}}_{3}{\mathrm{PO}}_{4})$ . Nonetheless, the low crystallinity of MOFs by the introduction of a strong acid makes it difficult to visualize the conducting pathway, although visualization of $\mathrm{H}^{+}$ hopping path or precise tuning (under subångström scale) of hopping pathways is the advantage in the MOF platform. In addition, it is difficult to avoid the degradation of conductivity. Those are still challenging in proton-conductive MOF studies. Therefore, highly stable host materials are extremely important in this approach.
(ii) The formation of a continuous H-bond network is another critical factor. Typically, the proton-conductive MOFs are significantly affected by humidity. Consequently, a continuous conducting pathway by H-bonding is easily broken under the low humidity condition, resulting in low proton conductivity. Furthermore, conducting media like $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ can be released by heating above the BP, which also leads to a deconstruction of the conducting pathway. Therefore, two kinds of approaches, a strong inherent H-bonding network with a hydrophilic channel and mining the alternative conducting media, have been extensively investigated. Moreover, a flexible structure which can maintain a proton conducting pathway through structural transformation depending on the humidity is required for future endeavors. In addition, the protonconducting performance at subzero temperature should be of significant concern for practical proton-conducting materials in the automotive applications of fuel cells in colder climates.
(iii) In fact, proton-conductive MOFs are useful for elucidating the conducting pathway with single-crystal analysis. However, the single-crystal conductivity study was limited, although there are a few interesting results. Furthermore, there are limited examples of computational studies for proton conduction mechanisms. No study was found for the prediction of potential proton-conductive MOFs through a highthroughput screening method. Moreover, unidirectional proton transport in MOFs through pH gradation control in the crystal could represent a future generation of proton-conductive MOFs. These fields still offer opportunities for further research. According to the considerable challenges such as mechanical strength, chemical stability, fuel crossover, processability in the form of film or membranes, oxidative stability against the redox reaction in the cell, and electrochemical evaluation with membrane electrode assembly, we conclude that future endeavors should be directed toward the durability of the structure at first and proton conductivity in MOFs with expansion of materials to organic and inorganic composites. Furthermore, computational study aids applicable to various materials for the conduction mechanism should be further developed. The overall efforts lead to the realization of realworld MOF-based PEM materials in the near future.
